textosLlegan("theory", {"fil-t2":{"html":"<blockquote class=\"src\">\n<p>How to study this topic: read each section slowly, underline the <strong>terms in bold</strong> (they are the vocabulary of the exam) and, at the end of each block, try to answer in your own words the question that heads it. The three comparison tables (nature/culture, dualism/monism and the historical conceptions) are the best guide for revision.</p> </blockquote>\n<h2 id=\"concepciones-filosóficas-del-ser-humano-a-lo-largo-de-la-historia\">1. Philosophical ways of understanding the human being throughout history</h2>\n<p>Each age has answered the question of the human being in its own way. Reviewing those answers helps us understand the present, because many of them are still alive in our way of thinking.</p>\n<p><strong>Five traditions.</strong> Before going through the different periods, a map is useful. The answers you are going to see can be grouped into five great traditions, that is, ways of thinking that are passed down from generation to generation and that still coexist in our culture:</p>\n<table> <thead> <tr> <th>Tradition</th> <th>What it says about the human being</th> <th>Key idea</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td><strong>Eastern</strong> (India, ancient religions)</td> <td>A body that dies and a soul that does not die and can be reborn in other bodies</td> <td>The soul in transit</td> </tr> <tr> <td><strong>Greek</strong></td> <td>What distinguishes us is reason, the <em>lógos</em>, and we live in community</td> <td>The rational and political animal</td> </tr> <tr> <td><strong>Judeo-Christian</strong></td> <td>A creature made by God in his image, with a unique value</td> <td>The person</td> </tr> <tr> <td><strong>Humanist</strong> (since the Renaissance)</td> <td>A being with dignity, who occupies the centre and makes himself</td> <td>Dignity</td> </tr> <tr> <td><strong>Scientific</strong> (since the nineteenth century)</td> <td>One more living being, studied by biology, psychology or sociology</td> <td>Nature</td> </tr> </tbody> </table>\n<p><strong>The problem of anthropocentrism.</strong> Several of these traditions share an idea: the human being (<em>ánthrōpos</em>) stands at the centre, and the rest of nature exists for him. In 1967, the American historian Lynn White Jr. published in the journal <em>Science</em> a much-debated article that sought in this attitude one of the roots of the ecological crisis. In 2000, the chemist Paul Crutzen proposed calling the <strong>Anthropocene</strong> the epoch in which human action has become a force capable of changing the planet’s climate and geology; in 2024, geologists declined to give it official status, but the word has stuck. The scientific tradition itself reminds us, at the same time, that we are just one more species, dependent on the others. Masters of nature, or a part of it with more power and, therefore, more responsibility? This is the underlying question of Sustainable Development Goal 13, climate action.</p>\n<p>In <strong>Antiquity</strong>, the Greeks agreed that what distinguishes us from other animals is <strong>reason</strong> (the <em>logos</em>): we are <strong>rational animals</strong>. <strong>Aristotle</strong> added another essential feature, our social nature: we need to live in community in order to develop and be fully human.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\">\n<p>“Man is by nature a social animal.” — Aristotle, <em>Politics</em></p> </blockquote>\n<p><strong>Political animal: speech makes us citizens.</strong> The sentence from the <em>Politics</em> is part of a longer argument (I 2, 1253a1-18). The city (<em>pólis</em>) exists by nature, and the human being is by nature a political animal (<em>zôon politikón</em>); whoever lives outside any city, not through bad luck but by nature, is “either a beast or a god” (1253a3-4 and 29). But bees live together too. What sets us apart? Speech: “The voice is a sign of what is painful and what is pleasant, and that is why other animals have it too; speech (<em>lógos</em>), on the other hand, serves to show what is useful and what is harmful, and therefore what is just and what is unjust” (1253a10-15, own translation). We are political not because we like being together, but because we can argue together about what is just. The same book has a dark side, which must be read critically. Aristotle holds that there are slaves by nature, people who “share in reason enough to perceive it, but not to possess it” (I 5, 1254b22-23), and that between man and woman “the one is by nature superior and the other inferior; the one rules and the other is ruled” (1254b13-14). Later he specifies: the slave has no capacity to deliberate at all; the woman has it, but without authority; the child has it, but incomplete (I 13, 1260a12-14). Calling a social hierarchy “natural” is a very effective way of shielding it from criticism. Something to think about: are there things we call “natural” today that may be no more than custom?</p>\n<p>In the <strong>Middle Ages</strong>, thought becomes <strong>theocentric</strong> (God at the centre). The human being is understood as a <strong>creature made “in the image and likeness” of God</strong>; from this comes its dignity, but also its dependence: life is oriented towards salvation. Saint Augustine and Saint Thomas Aquinas stand out here.</p>\n<p><strong>What Christianity changed.</strong> Christianity inherited from Plato the idea of an immortal soul, but transformed it in three ways. First, the soul is no longer something that passes from one body to another, as in the transmigration Plato believed in, but a <strong>personal</strong> soul, created for each individual and unrepeatable. Second, the body is not a prison but God’s work, and the Christian creed looks forward not to the soul’s escape but to the resurrection of the body. Third, dignity extends to everyone: if every human being is made “in the image of God” (Genesis 1:27), so is the slave as much as the emperor, and Saint Paul wrote that in Christ “there is neither Jew nor Greek, slave nor free, male nor female” (Galatians 3:28). From this root, and from the theological debates about the Trinity, comes the notion of the <strong>person</strong> that you will see in section 6 with Boethius. But there is another side. The doctrine of <strong>original sin</strong>, developed by Augustine, turned the body and its desires into suspect ground, and <strong>asceticism</strong> (fasting, chastity, mortification) was presented as a path to perfection; and equality before God coexisted for centuries with slavery and with the subjection of women. Another verse gave much food for thought: God tells the first humans to “fill the earth and subdue it” (Genesis 1:28). In 1967 the historian Lynn White blamed this idea of dominion for the ecological crisis; others reply that the same book asks humans to “till it and keep it”, speaking of the garden (Genesis 2:15), and Pope Francis, in the encyclical <em>Laudato si'</em> (2015), reads it as a duty of care. The debate remains open.</p>\n<p><strong>A text by Augustine: the path inwards.</strong> Augustine of Hippo (354-430) is the great bridge between Plato and Christianity. His <em>Confessions</em> (c. 397-400) open with a sentence addressed to God: “You made us for yourself, and our heart is restless until it rests in you” (I, 1, 1). And in <em>On True Religion</em> (c. 390) he gives a piece of advice that marked later philosophy: “Do not go outside; return into yourself: truth dwells in the inner human being” (39, 72; own translation of both texts). Guided commentary. 1) <strong>What is the human being according to the first text?</strong> A being made for something it lacks, and therefore always restless: no good in this world ever quite fills it. 2) <strong>Where does the second look for truth?</strong> Not outside, in nature, like the first Greek philosophers, but within one’s own inner life, where the soul finds God. 3) <strong>What does it anticipate?</strong> Against the sceptics, Augustine wrote “if I am mistaken, I exist” (<em>Si fallor, sum</em>; <em>The City of God</em>, XI, 26): to be mistaken, you have to exist. Twelve centuries later, Descartes would reach a very similar certainty. 4) <strong>Something to think about:</strong> is the restlessness Augustine describes only religious, or do you also recognise it when you get something you wanted very much and straight away want something else? Compare it with Schopenhauer, in section 8.</p>\n<p>With <strong>Modernity</strong> (16th–18th centuries), the centre shifts towards the human being and its capacity to know. <strong>Descartes</strong> defines the human being by <strong>thought</strong>: although he can doubt everything, he cannot doubt that he is thinking, and his whole philosophy rests on that certainty.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\">\n<p>“I think, therefore I am.” — René Descartes, <em>Discourse on Method</em> (1637)</p> </blockquote>\n<p><strong>A text by Descartes: the pilot and the ship.</strong> Descartes separated mind and body like no one else: the mind is pure “thing that thinks”, without extension, and the body pure extension, a machine like those of animals, which for him were automata (<em>Discourse on the Method</em>, 1637, Part V). But in the sixth of his <em>Meditations on First Philosophy</em> (1641) he admits that experience says something else: “Nature also teaches me, through these sensations of pain, hunger, thirst and so on, that I am not merely present in my body as a pilot is present in his ship, but that I am very closely joined to it and, as it were, intermingled with it, so that I form a single whole with it” (AT VII, 81, own translation). Guided commentary. 1) <strong>The image rejected:</strong> the pilot sees with his eyes that his ship has sprung a leak; I do not see that my foot is injured: it hurts. 2) <strong>The problem:</strong> if mind and body are two such different substances, how do they touch? Descartes proposed that they communicate in the pineal gland, at the centre of the brain (<em>The Passions of the Soul</em>, 1649, arts. 31-32). 3) <strong>The objection:</strong> in 1643 Princess Elisabeth of Bohemia asked him in a letter how something that takes up no space can push something that does, and Descartes never gave her an answer that convinced her. 4) <strong>Something to think about:</strong> when you are nervous before an exam and your stomach aches, is it your mind moving your body, or is it a single thing seen in two ways? That second possibility is Spinoza’s.</p>\n<p><strong>Spinoza: one thing seen in two ways.</strong> Baruch Spinoza (1632-1677) solved Descartes’s problem by denying its starting point. For him there is only one substance, God or Nature, and thought and extension are not two things, but two ways (attributes) in which that single reality expresses itself. That is why he writes in the <em>Ethics</em> (published in 1677, after his death) that “the mind and the body are one and the same thing, conceived now under the attribute of thought, now under that of extension” (III, proposition 2, scholium). The mind does not govern the body, nor the body the mind: what happens in one happens in the other, like the same sentence spoken in two languages. Hence, in the same scholium, a provocative sentence: “nobody has yet determined what a body can do”. Guided commentary. 1) <strong>Which problem disappears?</strong> That of interaction: there is no need for a pineal gland, because there are not two things to connect. 2) <strong>What is gained?</strong> A view of the body without contempt: every being strives to persevere in its being (the <em>conatus</em>, III, 6), and joy is the passage to a greater perfection, that is, to a greater power of acting; sadness, to a lesser one. 3) <strong>What is lost?</strong> If mind and body follow the laws of Nature, our freedom cannot consist in choosing without causes; for Spinoza, being free means understanding the causes of what we feel. You can carry on in the Affects section.</p>\n<p><strong>Hobbes and Rousseau: what would we be like without society?</strong> In the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries, the question “what is the human being?” was posed through a thought experiment: imagining the <strong>state of nature</strong>, life without a State, without laws and without a shared culture. Thomas Hobbes, in <em>Leviathan</em> (1651), concludes that it would be a war “of all against all”. Not because we are monsters, but for three reasons: competition for the same goods, distrust of others and the desire for glory; and since we are roughly equal in strength, no one is safe. In that situation there is no farming, no arts, no science, and human life is “solitary, poor, nasty, brutish, and short” (ch. 13). The way out is a covenant by which everyone hands over their power to a sovereign. Jean-Jacques Rousseau, in the <em>Discourse on the Origin and Basis of Inequality among Men</em> (1755), turns this upside down: natural man lives alone, healthy and without malice, guided by self-love and by pity, a natural reluctance to see another suffer; and what distinguishes him is perfectibility, the capacity to change. What corrupts him is society, and in particular property: “The first man who, having enclosed a piece of land, took it into his head to say ‘this is mine’, and found people simple enough to believe him, was the true founder of civil society” (Part II). Neither of them describes a real past: these are hypotheses for separating what in us comes from nature from what comes from culture. Something to think about: if human beings are selfish by nature, how can pity be explained? And if they are good by nature, where does the society that corrupts them come from?</p>\n<p>Later, in the Enlightenment, <strong>Kant</strong> stresses that the human being is a <strong>rational, free and autonomous</strong> being, endowed with a <strong>dignity</strong> that makes it an end in itself and never a mere means.</p>\n<p>In fifth-century BC Athens, the Sophists and other thinkers put into circulation a distinction that we still use. On the one hand, <strong><em>phýsis</em></strong>: the natural, the innate, what occurs necessarily and is the same everywhere; fire burns the same in Greece as in Persia. On the other, <strong><em>nómos</em></strong>: the norm or custom, what is learned, changes over time and is proper to each people.</p><p>With this distinction everything was discussed. <strong>Protagoras</strong> defended the value of laws and customs: although they are conventional, without them there would be no common life. <strong>Antiphon</strong> maintained that human laws are added agreements, whereas what nature commands imposes itself without remedy. And the <strong>Callicles</strong> whom Plato presents in the <em>Gorgias</em> took the idea to the extreme: according to nature, what is just would be that the stronger has more, and laws would be an invention of the weak to restrain him.</p><p>The historian <strong>Herodotus</strong> recounts in his <em>Histories</em> (III, 38) that the Persian king Darius asked some Greeks for how much money they would eat the corpse of their father, and they were horrified; then he asked some Indians who did do so for how much they would burn their fathers, as the Greeks did, and they were the ones scandalised. Each people believes that its customs are the best.</p><p>Taking off one's shoes when entering a house, greeting with two kisses or with a bow is <em>nómos</em>; needing to eat and sleep is <em>phýsis</em>. But not everything is classified so easily. What about language? We have a natural capacity to speak, but each language is conventional. Plato devoted an entire dialogue to this doubt, the <em>Cratylus</em>.</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: does justice or inequality between people belong to nature or to convention? What would change if they were one thing or the other?</p>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t2-41\">In the <strong>contemporary period</strong> (19th–21st centuries), the answers multiply. <strong>Marx</strong> insists that we are <strong>social</strong> beings who make ourselves through <strong>labour</strong>; and he denounces <strong>alienation</strong>, the situation in which, under an unjust system, the worker is turned into a commodity and separated from what he produces and from himself. <strong>Nietzsche</strong>, after proclaiming “the death of God” (the end of traditional values), proposes a human being capable of creating its own values, the <strong>overman</strong> (Übermensch), and describes that path with three images: the camel (which bears its duties), the lion (which rebels) and the child (which creates and plays freely). <strong>Freud</strong>, with <strong>psychoanalysis</strong> (1900–1930), holds that a good part of what we desire and do arises from the <strong>unconscious</strong>: the ego ‘is not master in its own house’. Finally, the <strong>existentialism</strong> of <strong>Sartre</strong> and <strong>Simone de Beauvoir</strong> maintains that the human being has no essence fixed in advance: first it exists and then, through its decisions, it gradually makes itself. We are, above all, <strong>freedom</strong> and <strong>project</strong>.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\">\n<p>“Existence precedes essence.” — Jean-Paul Sartre</p> </blockquote>\n<p><strong>A text by Marx: the human essence lies outside the individual.</strong> In 1845 Karl Marx jotted down eleven short theses against the philosopher Ludwig Feuerbach, which Engels published in 1888. The sixth says: “The human essence is no abstraction inherent in each single individual. In its reality, it is the ensemble of social relations” (<em>Theses on Feuerbach</em>, VI, own translation). Guided commentary. 1) <strong>Whom is he writing against?</strong> Against the whole tradition that looked for what is human inside each person: a soul, a reason, a religious feeling, the same in everyone and in every age. 2) <strong>What does he propose?</strong> That we are what we do together, and above all how we produce: being human as a slave in Athens, as a medieval serf or as a delivery rider for a digital platform is not the same thing. That is why he wrote with Engels that “it is not consciousness that determines life, but life that determines consciousness” (<em>The German Ideology</em>, 1845-1846). 3) <strong>Why work?</strong> In the <em>Economic and Philosophic Manuscripts of 1844</em> he draws a distinction: the animal produces only what it or its young need; the human being also produces when free from need, and even according to the laws of beauty. It is in work that we become human, which is why alienation is so serious: what we produce turns against us. 4) <strong>Something to think about:</strong> if the human essence changes with social relations, is there anything human left that is common to all ages? What would Aristotle say to you?</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: in <em>Capital</em> (1867), Marx explains that under capitalism things become <strong>commodities</strong>, objects made to be sold, and that relationships between people end up looking like relationships between things. What would Marx say about Black Friday? Write a short dialogue between Marx and someone queuing at six in the morning to buy a television.</p>\n<p><strong>A text by Nietzsche: something that must be overcome.</strong> In the prologue to <em>Thus Spoke Zarathustra</em> (1883), the prophet Zarathustra comes down from the mountain and announces in the market square: “I teach you the overman. Man is something that shall be overcome. What have you done to overcome him?” (§ 3). And a little later: “Man is a rope stretched between the animal and the overman: a rope over an abyss” (§ 4, own translation). Guided commentary, with care, because this text is very often misread. 1) <strong>What is the human being here?</strong> Not a fixed essence, but a crossing, something halfway along the road. 2) <strong>What is the overman?</strong> Not a race, nor an individual stronger than the rest, but someone who, after the “death of God”, is able to create their own values and to say yes to life as it is. 3) <strong>What is the opposite danger?</strong> Zarathustra describes the “last man”, who no longer desires anything great, seeks only his small comforts and, he says, “blinks” (§ 5). The crowd in the square, on hearing this, clamours to be given this last man. 4) <strong>Something to think about:</strong> does the “last man” resemble a life devoted only to being comfortable and entertained? Is overcoming yourself the same as competing with others?</p>\n<p><strong>Sartre and Beauvoir: freedom in a situation.</strong> In <em>Existentialism Is a Humanism</em> (1946), Sartre explains his formula with an object: a paper knife. Whoever makes it knows in advance what it is for; in its case, the essence (the plan) comes before existence. With human beings it is the other way round: if there is no God who thought of them beforehand, they first exist and then define themselves by what they do. That is why, he says, man “is condemned to be free”: he did not choose to be free, and he cannot stop choosing, not even when he says he is not choosing. Simone de Beauvoir applied this idea to the condition of women. Her best-known sentence opens the second volume of <em>The Second Sex</em> (1949): “One is not born, but rather becomes, a woman”. She does not deny that there are different bodies; she denies that the body is a destiny. What a society understands by “being a woman” (keeping quiet, caring, pleasing) is learnt and imposed, just as it was once presented as “natural”, remember Aristotle, that women should obey. Beauvoir adds something Sartre had left in the background: freedom is always in a <strong>situation</strong>, with a body, a class, an era and laws that make it easier or harder. Later, Judith Butler (<em>Gender Trouble</em>, 1990) took the idea further: the way we live our gender is also built by acting, over and over again. Something to think about: which things in your life are you “born” and which do you “become”?</p>\n<p><strong>Transcendence and immanence.</strong> To explain why that situation weighs more heavily on women, Beauvoir uses two words. <strong>Transcendence</strong> is launching oneself towards one's own projects that open up a future: learning a trade, writing a book, changing something in the world. <strong>Immanence</strong> is being trapped in tasks that repeat themselves without leaving anything new behind, like the meal that will have to be made again tomorrow. What Beauvoir denounces is not that those tasks exist, but that culture has reserved transcendence for men and has given women immanence as if it were their nature. In Year 2 <em>The Second Sex</em> is one of the PAU texts, in the topic on <a href=\"#teoria/hf-beauvoir\">Simone de Beauvoir</a>.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr> <th>Period</th> <th>Author(s)</th> <th>Idea of the human being</th> </tr> </thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr> <td>Antiquity</td> <td>Aristotle</td> <td>Rational and social animal; body and soul united</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td>Middle Ages</td> <td>St Augustine, St Thomas</td> <td>Creature in the image of God; life oriented towards salvation (theocentrism)</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td>Modernity</td> <td>Descartes</td> <td>Thinking subject (<em>res cogitans</em>): reason defines the self</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td>Modernity (Enlightenment)</td> <td>Kant</td> <td>Rational, free and autonomous being, with <strong>dignity</strong>: an end in itself</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td>Contemporary</td> <td>Marx</td> <td>Social being who produces itself through <strong>labour</strong></td> </tr>\n<tr> <td>Contemporary</td> <td>Nietzsche</td> <td>Being that must overcome itself and create its own values (<strong>overman</strong>: not a biologically superior being, but one who overcomes the morality of the herd)</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td>Contemporary</td> <td>Freud</td> <td>Being driven largely by the <strong>unconscious</strong>: the ego is not master in its own house</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td>Contemporary</td> <td>Sartre, Beauvoir</td> <td>Free being with no prior essence: a <strong>project</strong> that makes itself</td> </tr> </tbody> </table>\n<p><strong>Are we our tools too?</strong> The latest answer to the question about the human being comes from technology. Since the first stone axe we have never lived without tools, and perhaps technology is not something added to human nature but part of it: an animal with few instincts has to build itself a world. Today that relationship is closer than ever. In 1998 the philosophers Andy Clark and David Chalmers put forward the thesis of the <strong>extended mind</strong>: they imagined Otto, a man with Alzheimer’s who writes everything down in a notebook and checks it to find out where a museum is, just as someone else consults their memory. If the notebook does the same job as the memory, why not count it as part of his mind? Earlier, in 1985, Donna Haraway had published her <em>A Cyborg Manifesto</em>: the cyborg, a blend of organism and machine, allowed her to attack the old hierarchical pairs that we saw emerge with Plato (nature and culture, human and animal, man and woman). Critics warn of the opposite: if technology is part of us, it can also dominate us, and apps designed to capture our attention are a good example. Something to think about: your phone keeps your photos, your contacts and your diary. Is it part of your mind, like Otto’s notebook? And if you lose it, do you lose something of yourself?</p>\n<p><strong>Comparative table: ten answers to “what is the human being?”.</strong> To revise, look at two things in each author: how body and mind are related, and what makes us human.</p><ul><li><strong>Plato:</strong> dualism; the soul, immortal and in three parts, is the true self, and the body its prison. What is human is reason governing the desires.</li><li><strong>Aristotle:</strong> hylomorphism; the soul is the form of the body and does not exist without it. We are rational and political animals, because speech lets us argue about what is just; but he calls slavery and the subjection of women “natural”.</li><li><strong>Christianity (Augustine):</strong> a personal, immortal soul and a body created by God and called to rise again; every person has dignity as the image of God, and truth is sought within.</li><li><strong>Descartes:</strong> two substances, mind and body-machine; the self is defined by thought, and its union with the body remains a problem.</li><li><strong>Spinoza:</strong> a single reality with two attributes; mind and body are the same thing, and freedom consists in understanding the causes of our affects.</li><li><strong>Hobbes and Rousseau:</strong> the state of nature as a hypothesis: rival beings who need a covenant to live in peace (Hobbes), or good, perfectible beings corrupted by society (Rousseau).</li><li><strong>Marx:</strong> the human essence is the ensemble of social relations; we make ourselves by working.</li><li><strong>Nietzsche:</strong> the human being is a crossing, something to be overcome by creating one’s own values.</li><li><strong>Sartre and Beauvoir:</strong> first we exist and then we make ourselves; freedom is always in a situation, and the body is not a destiny.</li><li><strong>Technology:</strong> perhaps our tools are part of what we are, and the boundary between organism and machine becomes blurred.</li></ul>\n<p>Among the conceptions of the human being there is one born in the <strong>Renaissance</strong> that still resonates: that of the human <strong>without a fixed nature</strong>. In his <em>Oration on the Dignity of Man</em> (1486), <strong><a href=\"#biografias/pico\">Giovanni Pico della Mirandola</a></strong> (1463-1494) imagines God telling Adam that, unlike the other creatures, he has given him neither a fixed place nor a fixed form: it will be he who, by his <strong>freedom</strong>, gives himself the form he prefers, toward the beast or toward the divine. It is one of the first formulations of a very modern idea: that the human being “is” not something once and for all, but rather <strong>makes himself</strong> (the same intuition that, centuries later, existentialism will push to the extreme: “first one exists and then defines oneself”).</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\">\n<p>I have given you, O Adam, neither a fixed place, nor a form of your own, nor any gift that is yours alone, so that the place, the form and the gifts you yourself choose, you may have and possess by your own decision. […] You, by contrast, constrained by no limit, shall determine it according to your own will. — Pico della Mirandola, <em>Oration on the Dignity of Man</em> (1486)</p> </blockquote>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: can we really “make” ourselves, or are we born already with limits (the body, the language, the family, the age) that we do not choose? You will study Pico in Year 2, in the Renaissance; here it is one more answer to the question “what is the human being?”.</p>\n<p>To go further: in the reading <a href=\"#lecturas/fil-lec-pico-darwin\">‘Pico and Darwin: what place does the human being occupy in nature?’</a>, Pico’s oration (1486) is read alongside the final pages of Darwin’s <em>The Descent of Man</em> (1871). Notice a difference that Pico does not underline: his human being is free because a creator has made him a gift of that freedom, whereas for Sartre’s existentialism freedom is granted by no one. Darwin, for his part, places the human being within the history of the other animals, not above it.</p>\n<h2>2. Body and mind: are we body, soul, or both?</h2>\n<p>When we ask ourselves what we are made of, one of the classic problems of philosophy appears: the <strong>mind–body problem</strong> (or soul–body problem). Are we one thing or two? The answers fall into two great positions.</p>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t2-29\"><strong>Dualism</strong> holds that the human being is composed of <strong>two distinct realities</strong>: a material body and an immaterial soul (or mind), the latter being the more important and the one that constitutes our true essence. Its first great defender was <strong>Plato</strong>, for whom the soul is immortal and the <strong>body</strong> is a kind of “prison” that confines it and hinders it on its way to knowledge and the good. Plato explained this with the <strong>myth of the winged chariot</strong>: a charioteer (reason) guides two horses, one noble and one unruly (the passions and desires), and his task is to master them. Hence his idea of a soul with three parts: <strong>rational</strong>, <strong>spirited</strong> and <strong>appetitive</strong>. Centuries later, <strong>Descartes</strong> renewed dualism by distinguishing two substances: the <strong>res cogitans</strong> (“thinking thing”, the mind) and the <strong>res extensa</strong> (“extended thing”, the body and matter), which he conceived almost as a machine.</p>\n<p><strong>A text by Plato: the body as a hindrance.</strong> In the <em>Phaedo</em>, Socrates spends his last day in prison, before drinking the poison, and explains to his friends why he is not afraid of death. This is the heart of his reasoning: “As long as we have a body and our soul is kneaded together with such an evil, we shall never fully gain what we desire, and that is the truth. The body gives us a thousand chores because of the food it needs; if illnesses attack us, they get in the way of our hunt for what is; and it fills us with loves, desires, fears, images of every kind and a great deal of nonsense, so that, because of it, we never manage to think anything at all” (Plato, <em>Phaedo</em>, 66b-c, own translation). Guided commentary. 1) <strong>Thesis:</strong> the body is an obstacle to knowing the truth. 2) <strong>Reasons:</strong> its needs distract us, its illnesses stop us and its passions confuse us; a little later he adds that even wars arise from the desire for the wealth the body demands (66c-d). 3) <strong>Consequence:</strong> if the soul knows better the further it moves away from the body, then philosophising, as Socrates says, is “practising for death” (67e), and death is the soul’s release. 4) <strong>Assessment:</strong> he has a point: when we are hungry, sleepy or frightened, it is hard to think. But could we know anything without eyes or ears? That will be Aristotle’s objection.</p>\n<p><strong>Why three parts? The argument from conflict.</strong> In Book IV of the <em>Republic</em>, Plato does not simply state that the soul has parts: he argues for it. He starts from a principle: the same thing cannot do or undergo opposites at the same time and in the same respect (436b). Now, sometimes someone is thirsty and yet does not drink, because they know the water will make them ill (439c). If something in them urges them to drink and something holds them back, these cannot be the same thing: one is <strong>desire</strong> (the appetitive part, <em>epithymētikón</em>), the other <strong>reason</strong> (<em>logistikón</em>). For the third part, Plato tells an anecdote. A man called Leontius was walking past the corpses of some executed criminals; he felt an urge to look at them and, at the same time, disgust at himself; in the end he ran towards them, shouting at his own eyes: “There you are, you wretches, take your fill of the fine sight!” (439e-440a). That anger against one’s own desire is neither reason nor appetite: it is <strong>spirit</strong> (the spirited part, <em>thymoeidés</em>), which grows indignant and which, when well educated, sides with reason. In the <em>Timaeus</em>, Plato places the three in the head, the chest and the belly. A person’s justice consists in each part doing its own job: reason rules, spirit supports it and the desires obey (441d-e). A point that is often forgotten: in the <em>Phaedo</em>, the soul is simple and indivisible, and that is why it is immortal (78b-80b). So many interpreters read the three parts of the <em>Republic</em> not as three souls, but as three forces within a single one.</p>\n<p><strong>The dualism we carry in our language.</strong> Plato lived twenty-four centuries ago, but his way of splitting the human being in two is still alive in phrases we say without thinking. “She gave herself to it body and soul.” “His body is here, but his head is somewhere else.” “Don’t be led by your heart: think with your head.” “That day I found out what he was really like”, as if an authentic self were hidden behind what we see. “The body dies, but the soul does not.” “Don’t be an animal!” “We must master nature.” Notice that these are not just pairs of opposites: in each one, one side is worth more than the other. The soul more than the body; reason more than emotion; the human being more than the animal; culture more than nature. And on the list there is another pair that has done a great deal of harm, man and woman, when reason was assigned to men and the body and feelings to women (“women are more emotional; men, more rational”). Plato himself is ambiguous: in the <em>Republic</em> (V, 455d-456a) he admits women among the guardians of the city, but in the <em>Timaeus</em> (90e-91a) he imagines that cowardly men are reborn as women. Present-day philosophy is suspicious of this scheme: rather than describing what we are like, it sets out who gives orders to whom. Try it: look for three more expressions, in Basque or in Spanish, in which one side of the pair is worth more than the other, and ask yourself what would change if we valued both equally.</p>\n<p>Against this view, <strong>monism</strong> (often in its <strong>materialist</strong> form) holds that there is only <strong>one</strong> reality: we are body, and what we call mind or soul is not a separate substance, but the activity of that body, above all of the brain. In this line stand <strong>Democritus</strong> in Antiquity (everything is atoms and void), <strong>Hobbes</strong> and <strong>La Mettrie</strong> in Modernity (the latter wrote <em>Man a Machine</em>, 1747) and, today, <strong>Daniel Dennett</strong> or <strong>eliminativists</strong> such as Paul and Patricia Churchland, as well as a good part of neuroscience. An intermediate and very influential position was that of <strong>Aristotle</strong>, who rejected separating soul and body: for him the soul is the <strong>form</strong> of the living body, its principle of life, and cannot exist without it (he distinguished a vegetative, a sensitive and a rational soul).</p>\n<p><strong>A text by Aristotle: the soul as the form of the body.</strong> In <em>On the Soul</em>, Aristotle defines the soul as “the first actuality of a natural body that has life potentially” (II 1, 412a27-28). Put more simply: the soul is not a thing placed inside the body, but what makes that body alive and work as what it is. He clarifies this with two examples: “That is why we should not ask whether the soul and the body are one thing, any more than we ask it of the wax and the shape stamped on it. […] If the eye were an animal, its soul would be sight; without it, the eye would no longer be an eye except in name, like an eye made of stone or a painted one” (II 1, 412b6-22, own translation, abridged). Guided commentary. 1) <strong>Thesis:</strong> the soul is the <strong>form</strong> (<em>morphḗ</em>) and the body the <strong>matter</strong> (<em>hýlē</em>) of one and the same being: hence the name <strong>hylomorphism</strong>. 2) <strong>Examples:</strong> wax and its shape cannot be separated; sight is what the eye does when it works. 3) <strong>Consequences:</strong> to die is to lose that organisation, which is why a corpse is a human body only in name; the self is not the soul, but the whole compound; and there is no transmigration, because a soul cannot enter just any body, just as the carpenter’s craft cannot slip into flutes (I 3, 407b20-26). 4) <strong>Knowing:</strong> at birth, the mind is like a writing tablet on which nothing has yet been written (III 4, 430a1): everything begins with the senses. An honest warning: in III 5, Aristotle speaks of a “separable” intellect, and interpreters have argued for centuries about what he meant.</p>\n<p>In the 1960s, the American neuroscientist Paul MacLean proposed a very popular model, that of the <strong>triune brain</strong>, which he developed in <em>The Triune Brain in Evolution</em> (1990). According to him, our brain would be like three superimposed brains that appeared over the course of evolution:</p><ul><li>the <strong>reptilian brain</strong>, the oldest: basic instincts, survival, defence of territory;</li><li>the <strong>limbic system</strong>: emotions, affective bonds, group life and care of offspring;</li><li>the <strong>neocortex</strong>, the most recent: reason, language, imagination and planning.</li></ul><p>Imagine the eve of a difficult exam. Fear knots your stomach (limbic), you feel like closing your notes and fleeing to anything else (reptilian), and at the same time a part of you organises a plan: review two topics and sleep well (neocortex). The model helps to see that conflict. But it should be taken as a <strong>simplification</strong>: current neuroscience knows that these areas are not independent layers, but rather work connected, and that emotions also intervene in reasoning.</p><p>Biology teaches us something else: all current human beings form <strong>a single species</strong>, <em>Homo sapiens</em>, and we are genetically very similar; moreover, most variation occurs between individuals within the same group, not between groups. That is why the concept of <strong>“race”</strong> applied to humans is not scientifically adequate: skin colour is a superficial trait that does not mark clear boundaries. However, the word has been used throughout history to justify slavery, segregation or extermination, that is, with an ideological use.</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: why insist on differences of “race” if biologically we are a single species? What interests might lie behind this?</p>\n<p>The view of the body has changed greatly through history: despised by Platonism, it was valued by Aristotle; in the <strong>Middle Ages</strong> it was at once “temple” and “tomb”: a temple created by God and called to be resurrected, but also flesh that tempts and is to be distrusted (Saint Paul, Saint Augustine); and the <strong>Renaissance</strong> recovered a positive and dignified image of the human body, though not uniformly, since it coexisted with the Counter-Reformation and with new forms of asceticism. All this forms the so-called <strong>psychosomatic structure</strong> of the person: the union of the psychic (<em>psyche</em>) and the bodily (<em>soma</em>) that today we tend to understand as a whole.</p>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t2-32\">In today’s philosophy of mind the debate is more fine-grained. The <strong>identity theory</strong> holds that each mental state <em>is</em> a state of the brain; <strong>functionalism</strong> defines the mind by what it does (processing information), whatever its physical basis, like a programme that could run on another machine; <strong>emergentism</strong> asserts that the mind arises from the organisation of neurons without being reducible to them; and <strong>property dualism</strong> admits a single substance, the physical, with physical and mental properties. Positions that reduce the mind to the physical collide with the so-called <strong>hard problem of consciousness</strong> (Chalmers, 1995), which remains open: neuroscience still does not explain why brain activity is experienced ‘from the inside’.</p>\n<p>The map has more boxes. The <strong>logical behaviourism</strong> of Gilbert Ryle (<em>The Concept of Mind</em>, 1949) mocked dualism as a ‘ghost in the machine’: saying that someone is sad would be saying how they tend to behave. The <strong>eliminativism</strong> of Paul and Patricia Churchland goes further: ‘belief’ or ‘desire’ would be concepts from a mistaken folk theory that neuroscience will end up replacing, as chemistry replaced phlogiston. <strong>Epiphenomenalism</strong> accepts the mind but denies it any effects: for Thomas Huxley (1874), consciousness accompanies the brain as the steam whistle accompanies the locomotive, without moving it. <strong>Panpsychism</strong> proposes the opposite: all matter would have some minimal degree of experience. And the <strong>mysterianism</strong> of Colin McGinn (1989) holds that perhaps our mind is not made to solve this problem, just as a dog cannot understand physics.</p>\n<table> <thead> <tr> <th>Position</th> <th>What it gains</th> <th>What it costs</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>Dualism</td> <td>It respects the experience of having an inner life</td> <td>Explaining how something immaterial moves a body</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Identity theory</td> <td>It fits with neuroscience</td> <td>Explaining how an octopus, with a different brain, also feels pain</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Functionalism</td> <td>It allows minds in other substrates</td> <td>Explaining why processing information feels like something ‘from the inside’</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Eliminativism</td> <td>It is the most economical</td> <td>Denying something that seems obvious: that we believe and desire</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Epiphenomenalism</td> <td>It leaves physics untouched</td> <td>Explaining what consciousness is for, then</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Panpsychism</td> <td>It avoids experience appearing out of nothing</td> <td>Explaining how many minimal experiences make up a mind</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Mysterianism</td> <td>It is modest</td> <td>Giving up on an answer</td> </tr> </tbody> </table>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr> <th></th> <th><strong>Dualism</strong></th> <th><strong>Monism / materialism</strong></th> </tr> </thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr> <td>What are we made of?</td> <td>Of two realities: body (material) and soul or mind (immaterial)</td> <td>Of a single reality: we are body; the mind is its activity</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td>Body–mind relationship</td> <td>The soul is superior and can exist without the body</td> <td>The mental depends on the physical; there is no separable soul</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td>The body is…</td> <td>Prison of the soul (Plato) or machine (Descartes)</td> <td>An essential part of what we are</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td>Is there anything after death?</td> <td>The soul can survive (immortality)</td> <td>With the death of the body everything ends</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td>Representatives</td> <td>Plato, Descartes</td> <td>Democritus, Hobbes, La Mettrie; today, Dennett, the Churchlands and much of neuroscience</td> </tr> </tbody> </table>\n<h3>Emotions: the body that evaluates</h3>\n<p>Where we can best see that we are a <strong>psychosomatic unity</strong> (from <em>psykhḗ</em>, soul, and <em>sôma</em>, body) is in the <strong>emotions</strong>. Imagine that at night you hear a bang in the kitchen when you thought you were alone at home. In a second, your heart races, your attention fixes on the noise, you feel a knot in your stomach and the urge to keep still or to run away. An emotion brings all of that together: a change in the body, an experience that is felt, an evaluation of the situation (there is danger) and a tendency to act. Darwin, in <em>The Expression of the Emotions in Man and Animals</em> (1872), showed that many of them are inherited responses that helped us to survive and that, in addition, tell others what is happening to us: a face of disgust warns the group not to touch that food.</p>\n<p>It is worth not confusing three words. An <strong>emotion</strong> is brief and intense and has an object: I get angry <em>with</em> someone, I am frightened <em>of</em> something. A <strong>mood</strong> lasts longer, is more diffuse and we do not always know what causes it: there are days when we are irritable for no clear reason. And a <strong>feeling</strong> is the emotion lived consciously and over time, already thought about and named: affection for a friend or a grudge carried for years. The neuroscientist Antonio Damasio (<em>Looking for Spinoza</em>, 2003) sums it up like this: the emotion happens first in the body, and the feeling is the perception that the mind forms of that state of the body.</p>\n<p>Are we born with emotions or do we learn them? In the 1960s and 1970s, Paul Ekman showed photographs of faces to people from many cultures, including the Fore of Papua New Guinea, who had had hardly any contact with the outside world, and almost all of them recognised the same expressions. Hence his thesis of universal <strong>basic emotions</strong>: joy, sadness, fear, anger, disgust and surprise. The psychologist Lisa Feldman Barrett (<em>How Emotions Are Made: The Secret Life of the Brain</em>, 2017) defends the opposite: the brain <strong>constructs</strong> each emotion with the concepts we have learnt in our language and our culture. One piece of evidence in her favour is that some languages have words for emotions that others do not name, such as Portuguese <em>saudade</em>, that longing for what has been lost or perhaps never was.</p>\n<p><strong>The wheel of emotions.</strong> The American psychologist Robert Plutchik proposed in 1980 that emotions be arranged like colours. His <strong>wheel</strong>, drawn like a flower, has eight basic emotions in opposing pairs: joy and sadness, trust and disgust, fear and anger, surprise and anticipation. Each has degrees of intensity (from serenity to ecstasy, from annoyance to rage), and neighbouring emotions blend to give complex emotions: joy and trust produce love; anticipation and joy, optimism; surprise and sadness, disappointment. The model is more a map than a proof, and other psychologists count the basic emotions differently, beginning with Ekman’s six. But it serves a practical purpose: giving a precise name to what we feel, which is the first step towards understanding it.</p>\n<p>What exactly is an emotion? In 1884 William James gave a surprising answer: we do not tremble because we are afraid; we are afraid because we notice that we are trembling. The emotion would be the <strong>perception of changes in the body</strong>. In 1927 Walter Cannon objected that very similar bodily changes accompany different emotions, such as fear and anger. The other great family of theories, which goes back to the Stoics, sees the emotion as a <strong>judgement</strong>: Martha Nussbaum (<em>Upheavals of Thought</em>, 2001) argues that grieving for someone’s death is judging that this person was important to my life. If so, emotions can be right or wrong, and they can be corrected. But some emotions resist what we think: someone who knows that flying is safer than driving still feels afraid at take-off. That case seems to prove James right, although cognitivists reply that there is an evaluation there too, only faster than judgement.</p>\n<p>The question of whether emotions are body or judgement is very ancient. Plato already located the forces of the soul in the body: in the <em>Timaeus</em>, reason dwells in the head, the <em>thymós</em> (spirit, the force that grows indignant and dares) in the chest, near the heart, and the desire for food and pleasure in the belly. But he did not always say the same thing. In the <em>Phaedo</em>, the soul is simple and it is the body that disturbs it with its needs and fears; in the <em>Republic</em> and the <em>Phaedrus</em>, the conflict lies within the soul, between reason, spirit and desire, like that between a charioteer and his two horses. Many interpreters read these three ‘parts’ as functions of a single soul, not as three souls. That map remains in our language: we say someone has ‘a cool head’, that they do something ‘from the heart’, or that something ‘comes from the gut’. Aristotle joined the two perspectives. In <em>On the Soul</em> (I, 1) he explains that anger can be described in two ways: the physicist will say it is the blood boiling around the heart; the dialectician, that it is the desire to return an injury. Both descriptions are true at once, because emotion is a movement of the body with the form of a judgement. And in the <em>Rhetoric</em> (II, 2) he defines it precisely: anger is a desire, accompanied by pain, to take revenge for a slight that one believes undeserved.</p>\n<p>In the modern era, the discussion centred on power: does reason rule the passions, or is it the other way round? Descartes responded, in his last work, <em>The Passions of the Soul</em> (1649), to the questions that Elisabeth of Bohemia had put to him by correspondence. For him, the passions are perceptions of the soul caused by the movements of the ‘animal spirits’, extremely subtle particles in the blood, and he distinguishes six primitive ones: wonder, love, hatred, desire, joy and sadness. They are not negative: they incline us to desire what benefits us. Nevertheless, they must be governed, and Descartes places his trust in habit: just as a hunting dog is trained to stay still when it sees a partridge instead of pouncing on it, there is no soul so weak that, properly guided, it cannot come to master its passions completely (article 50). Roughly a century later, David Hume turned that hierarchy upside down. In <em>A Treatise of Human Nature</em> (Book II, Part 3, Section 3) he argues that reason, on its own, never provokes action: it shows us what is true and which means lead to which ends, but the goal is always set by a desire. From this idea comes his most provocative remark: ‘Reason is, and ought only to be the slave of the passions’. Even more scandalous is this other one: it is not contrary to reason to choose the annihilation of the entire world rather than a scratch on my finger. For Hume, irrationality can only consist in a false belief, never in a desire.</p>\n<p>Between the Stoics and Spinoza there are two ways of freeing oneself from the passions that are worth comparing (you will see the Stoics in detail in Topic 5). For the Stoa, a passion (<em>páthos</em>) is a disturbance born of a false judgement: believing that something external, such as money or the opinion of others, is truly a good or an evil. They distinguished four basic passions: pleasure, pain, desire and fear. The goal was <em>apátheia</em>: to correct the judgement so that the passion disappears and only serene affections remain. Spinoza starts from a different idea of the human being. In the <em>Ethics</em> (part three) he defines affect as a change in body and mind that increases or diminishes our power of existing, our <em>conatus</em>, and reduces all affects to three primary ones: desire, joy and sadness. Love would be joy accompanied by the idea of an external cause; hatred, sadness accompanied by the idea of an external cause. We suffer a passion when we do not understand what affects us. That is why his remedy is not to suppress it, but to understand it: an affect that is a passion ceases to be one as soon as we form a clear and distinct idea of it (<em>Ethics</em> V, proposition 3). Put briefly: the Stoic eliminates the passion in order to be free; Spinoza understands it so that it stops dominating him.</p>\n<p>If emotions contain evaluations, they <strong>can also be educated</strong>. Aristotle already thought so: the virtuous person is not the one who feels no fear or anger, but the one who feels them when they should, about what they should and in the right measure, and this is learnt through habit, as one learns to play an instrument (you will see this in <a href=\"#teoria/fil-t5\">Topic 5</a>). Spinoza went further and devoted the third part of his <em>Ethics</em> to explaining the affects like any other natural phenomenon, in order to understand them and stop suffering them blindly; you can explore them in the <a href=\"#afectos\">Affects</a> section.</p>\n<p>In 1990, the psychologists Peter Salovey and John Mayer called <strong>emotional intelligence</strong> the capacity to perceive, understand and regulate one’s own and others’ emotions. The science journalist Daniel Goleman made it famous in 1995 with a hugely successful book, <em>Emotional Intelligence</em>, which divided it into five competences: knowing one’s own emotions, managing them, motivating oneself, recognising those of others (empathy) and handling relationships. Its subtitle promised to explain why it may matter more than the intelligence quotient. Criticism soon followed. Mayer himself warned that this promise had not been proven; self-assessment tests largely measure personality traits we already knew, such as emotional stability or agreeableness; and the sociologist Eva Illouz (<em>Cold Intimacies</em>, 2007) pointed out that companies turned the management of emotions into a tool for making workers more productive, a kind of ‘emotional capitalism’. Still, there remains an old idea that fashion did not invent: emotions can be educated. For Aristotle, through habit; for Spinoza, through understanding them. Psychology adds a simple resource: naming them. Distinguishing whether what you feel is anger, jealousy, shame or disappointment already changes what you can do with it.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: have you ever felt an emotion that you knew had no reason to exist, such as shame about something that was not your fault? Do you think that at that moment your body and your judgement were saying different things? If you could choose never to feel fear, would you choose it?</p>\n<h3>Desiring, willing and being a certain way</h3>\n<p>Alongside the emotions, our psychosomatic structure includes <strong>desire</strong>, the tendency towards something we lack or that attracts us, and the <strong>will</strong>, the capacity to decide what we do with our desires. They do not always go together. The poet Ovid put into Medea’s mouth a line that sums up the problem: I see the better and approve it, but I follow the worse. Aristotle called it <em>akrasía</em>, weakness of will. The philosopher Harry Frankfurt (1971) proposed that what is distinctive of a person is having <strong>second-order desires</strong>: desires about their own desires. Someone who wants to check their phone every few minutes and, at the same time, wishes they did not want to, is living that conflict, and free will would consist in the desire one identifies with winning out. To know all this we use <strong>introspection</strong>, looking inwards; but the psychologists Richard Nisbett and Timothy Wilson (1977) showed that we are often wrong when we explain why we chose something: we invent reasons that sound good.</p>\n<p><strong>Volition and motivation.</strong> Psychologists call <strong>volition</strong> the will in action: the capacity to set oneself a goal and sustain the effort until it is reached. It is not the same as <strong>motivation</strong>. Motivation is the desire, and desire rises and falls; volition is carrying on when the desire has gone. A distinction is made between primary motivations, linked to the needs of the body (hunger, sleep), and secondary ones, which are learnt (recognition, achievement). Volition rests on three capacities: <strong>self-control</strong>, restraining an impulse; the ability to <strong>delay gratification</strong>, which Walter Mischel studied from the 1960s by offering children one sweet straight away or two if they waited; and <strong>self-confidence</strong>, because those who believe they can try harder. Beware of hasty readings: a 2018 study showed that the result of Mischel’s test depends heavily on the family environment, not only on character. Are we born with willpower? Aristotle thought it is formed through habits: we become just by doing just things, just as we become cithara players by playing the cithara. Today it has a new enemy: according to the measurements of the psychologist Gloria Mark, in front of a screen we switch tasks, on average, in under a minute, and notifications have a lot to do with it.</p>\n<p>Is it possible to know what is best and not do it? Socrates denied it. In Plato’s <em>Protagoras</em> he argues that no one chooses evil knowingly: what we call ‘being overcome by pleasure’ is a miscalculation, like when a nearby object looks bigger than a distant one. The pleasure of now, being close, seems greater than the harm of tomorrow, and whoever could measure well would not make a mistake. Aristotle replied in book VII of the <em>Nicomachean Ethics</em> that <em>akrasia</em> exists, but that the knowledge of the akratic person is only partial knowledge: he has it as a drunk or a sleeping person has it, or as an actor recites verses he does not feel. He further distinguished two forms: that of the impetuous person, who acts without stopping to think, and that of the weak person, who thinks, decides well and then fails to hold to it. And he separated two figures we tend to confuse: the <strong>continent</strong> person (<em>enkratḗs</em>), who has bad desires but overcomes them, and the <strong>temperate</strong> person, who no longer has them. For Aristotle, the second is better: virtue is not a permanent struggle, but desiring well. There is also a practical way out, which the philosopher Jon Elster studied in <em>Ulysses and the Sirens</em> (1979): Ulysses, knowing he would not resist the song, asked to be tied to the mast. Turning off the phone before studying is the modern version of being tied to the mast.</p>\n<p><strong>True and manufactured needs.</strong> Human desire has two curious features. It is never exhausted: once one desire is fulfilled, another appears, and it almost always looks to the future, bound up with hope. And it can go against biology: some people go on hunger strike or give their lives for a cause, something no survival instinct explains. This plasticity also has a dark side. Theodor W. <a href=\"#biografias/adorno\">Adorno</a> and Max Horkheimer used the term <strong>culture industry</strong> (<em>Dialectic of Enlightenment</em>, 1947) for the system that mass-produces films, songs and fashions, and with them the tastes of those who consume them. Their fellow thinker Herbert Marcuse spoke of <strong>false needs</strong> (<em>One-Dimensional Man</em>, 1964): those that advertising imposes on us so that we keep buying and that, once satisfied, leave us just as we were. We need to eat; do we need the trainers from the latest advert? The question is not whether we desire, but who decides what we desire.</p>\n<p>Each person, moreover, has a stable way of feeling, thinking and acting: their <strong>personality</strong>. Traditionally, <strong>temperament</strong>, the inherited part, is distinguished from <strong>character</strong>, the part shaped by upbringing and experience. Present-day psychology describes personality with the <strong>Big Five</strong> model of traits, proposed by Paul Costa and Robert McCrae in the 1980s: openness to experience, conscientiousness, extraversion, agreeableness and emotional stability. Each trait is a scale, not a box: nobody is entirely ‘extravert’ or ‘introvert’. Twin studies indicate that around half of the differences between people is explained by heredity, and that traits change somewhat with age: we tend to become more conscientious and stable over the years. Whether character is as stable as it seems is exactly what the critics of virtue ethics debate (you will see this in <a href=\"#teoria/fil-t5\">Topic 5</a>).</p>\n<p>The word ‘temperament’ comes from ancient medicine. The Hippocratic treatises, especially <em>On the Nature of Man</em> (5th century BC), explained health as the balance of four <strong>humours</strong>: blood, phlegm, yellow bile and black bile. Galen (2nd century) spoke of the <em>krâsis</em>, the ‘mixture’ proper to each person, which in Latin was translated as <em>temperamentum</em>, and later tradition associated each humour with a type of character: the sanguine, cheerful and sociable; the phlegmatic, calm; the choleric, irritable, and the melancholic (from <em>mélaina kholḗ</em>, ‘black bile’), sad and reflective. The theory of the humours is false, but it left its mark on language (‘being in a good humour’) and on psychology: Kant still described the four temperaments in his <em>Anthropology</em> (1798), and Hans Eysenck placed them in the 20th century on two scales, extraversion and emotional stability. <strong>Character</strong>, by contrast, is made. Aristotle explained it with an etymology: <em>êthos</em>, character, comes from <em>éthos</em>, habit (<em>Nicomachean Ethics</em>, II, 1). We become just by practising just acts, as we become cithara players by playing the cithara, and habit, he says in the <em>Rhetoric</em> (I, 11), comes to resemble nature. Hence the expression ‘second nature’: what at first was hard ends up coming out on its own. Heraclitus had already summed it up in three words: <em>êthos anthrṓpōi daímōn</em>, ‘character is, for the human being, his destiny’.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: think of a goal you gave up. Did you lack motivation or volition? What would have helped you? Make a list of your five most important desires and separate those that respond to genuine needs from those that have come to you from outside. Does any of them guide your life?</p>\n<h2>3. The human being, an enigma to itself</h2>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t2-3\">Of all the beings we know, only one is able to stop and ask what it itself is: the human being. A stone does not wonder why it is a stone, and a cat does not reflect on what it means to be a cat. We, by contrast, not only live, but also ask ourselves what we are and what our actions mean. That is why it is said that the human being is both the <strong>subject</strong> and the <strong>object</strong> of this question: it is the one who investigates and, at the same time, the one investigated.</p>\n<p>In Topic 1 we saw that philosophy is born from <strong>wonder</strong>, from that capacity to be struck by what others find evident. Well, few things are closer and at the same time more mysterious than ourselves. The <strong>anthropological question</strong> —what is the human being?— is one of the oldest and most difficult in thought, and it is dealt with by one of the branches of philosophy: <strong>philosophical anthropology</strong> (from the Greek <em>ánthropos</em>, “human being”). It should not be confused with scientific or cultural anthropology, which describes peoples and their customs through observation; philosophical anthropology seeks, with concepts and arguments, what defines us and distinguishes us from other living beings.</p>\n<p><strong>The oldest clues.</strong> Nobody knows when someone first asked the question of what we are, but there are traces that point a very long way back. The first is <strong>burials</strong>: more than fifty thousand years ago, Neanderthals and Sapiens were already placing some of their dead in graves, sometimes with objects beside them. To bury someone is to treat the body as more than a remnant, and perhaps to wonder what remains of a person when they die. The second is <strong>cave art</strong>: in caves such as Altamira, Ekain or Santimamiñe, between thirty-five thousand and twelve thousand years ago, someone painted bison and horses that were not in front of them. This requires <strong>symbolic capacity</strong>, the faculty of making one thing (a line, a sound) stand for another. The third is <strong>myths</strong>: every people tells how human beings came into being, and each story carries an implicit answer. The clay to which a god gives breath, the children of a divine union or the animals that learnt to speak are already small anthropologies. Philosophy does not invent the question: it inherits it and turns it into a problem.</p>\n<p><strong>Kant's four questions.</strong> Immanuel Kant gave the anthropological question its most famous form. In the introduction to his <em>Logic</em> (1800, section III; Ak. IX 25) he says that the whole field of philosophy can be reduced to four questions: <em>what can I know?</em>, which metaphysics deals with; <em>what ought I to do?</em>, the question of morality; <em>what may I hope?</em>, the question of religion; and <em>what is the human being?</em>, the question of anthropology. And he adds at once that, at bottom, all of it could be counted as anthropology, because the first three refer to the last. The idea is simple and powerful: asking what we can know is asking about the limits of our reason; asking what we ought to do is asking about our freedom; and asking what we may hope is asking about our destiny. All three are really about us. You can check this in your own life: when you doubt a piece of news going round on your phone, you are asking yourself the first question; when you do not know whether to repeat what a friend told you in confidence, the second; when you wonder what will become of you in ten years, or whether anything survives death, the third. The first three already appeared in the <em>Critique of Pure Reason</em> (1781); the fourth, the one that gathers them together, is the one that gives this topic its meaning.</p>\n<p><strong>An existence that does not come ready-solved.</strong> Not all questions are philosophical. ‘How do I fix this plug?’ or ‘what time does the bus leave?’ arise from a practical need and are answered with a technique or a piece of data; once solved, they disappear. Philosophical questions are of another kind: ‘why do I exist?’, ‘what is it to be human?’, ‘what is the good?’, ‘what is truth?’. They do not arise from a passing difficulty but from a developed consciousness, and they do not die out when someone gives an answer. Behind them lies a feature of our condition: other animals are, so to speak, slotted into their world, whereas we, thanks to reason and consciousness, can call ourselves into question. This awareness of existing produces a <strong>philosophical unrest</strong>: human beings do not merely live; they realise that they are living and want to understand what meaning their life has. That is why philosophy is not a body of knowledge for getting out of a fix, as technology is, but an activity that asks about the whys and the ultimate meanings. This is the question that will close the topic, in section 8, and that Albert Camus put in the starkest way: is life worth living?</p>\n<p><strong>Two Greek ways of asking.</strong> Before Plato, the Greeks approached the human being by two paths. One was <strong>theoretical</strong>: to explain humans as one more part of nature. According to later testimonies, <a href=\"#biografias/anaximandro\">Anaximander</a> (6th century BC) held that the first humans could not have been born as they are born today, helpless for years, and that they formed inside creatures resembling fish; this was not a theory of evolution, but it was an attempt to explain our origin without resorting to the gods. The other path was <strong>practical</strong>: not so much what we are as how we ought to live. <a href=\"#biografias/heraclito\">Heraclitus</a> wrote that he had searched into himself (<em>edizēsámēn emeōutón</em>); <a href=\"#biografias/socrates\">Socrates</a> asked his fellow citizens what justice or courage is; the Sophists taught how to persuade in the assembly and debated whether norms are natural or conventional. At the temple of Apollo at Delphi, according to Pausanias, there was an inscription that sums up this second path: <em>gnôthi seautón</em>, ‘know thyself’. Socrates made it his own, and with it the anthropological question became a personal task.</p>\n<p>Answering is not simple, because we are complex beings: body and mind, biology and culture, individual and society, conditioning and freedom. Throughout history very different answers have been given and none has fully closed the debate. Already in Antiquity, the sophist <strong>Protagoras</strong> placed the human being at the centre of reflection with a famous sentence:</p>\n<p><strong>A subject, not only an object.</strong> Many textbooks open this topic with fossils and skulls, and turn it into a summary of what biology, palaeontology or psychology say. That knowledge is essential, but it does not answer the philosophical question. Science studies the human being as an <strong>object</strong>: it explains how we became <em>Homo sapiens</em>. Philosophy also thinks of it as a <strong>subject</strong>: the only being that asks about itself, about the meaning of what it does and about what it ought to become. So it is worth reading this topic with three cautions. First: science does not come <em>before</em> philosophy, but supplies it with material; when Aristotle calls the human being a <em>zôon politikón</em>, he is not classifying a species, but describing a being that shares speech and reasons with others. Second: explaining <strong>humanisation</strong> is not enough unless we also ask about <strong>humanism</strong>, that is, why we treat every human being as someone of value. Third: no scientific discourse is entirely neutral; behind some explanations lie philosophical assumptions (biological determinism, mechanistic materialism, essentialism) that have been used to justify racism or sexism, and which we should learn to recognise. Put as a formula: it is not enough to know how we became <em>Homo sapiens</em>; we need to understand why we are also <em>Homo quaerens</em>, the being that asks questions.</p>\n<p><strong>A being open to the world.</strong> The biologist Jakob von Uexküll (1864-1944) showed that every animal lives in its own <em>Umwelt</em>, its ‘surrounding world’: it does not perceive the whole of reality, only what matters to it for survival. His best-known example is the tick. It climbs onto a branch, and its world shrinks to almost three signals: the smell given off by a mammal's skin, which makes it let go; the warmth of the body, which tells it that it has landed well; and touch, which guides it to a hairless spot where it can dig in. Everything else, for the tick, does not exist. Max Scheler, in <em>The Human Place in the Cosmos</em> (1928), took up this idea to mark out the human difference. The animal is tied to its environment; the human being, by contrast, is <strong>open to the world</strong> (<em>Weltoffenheit</em>): it can look at things as objects, take an interest in what is of no use to it, such as a distant star or a prime number, and stand back from its own impulses. He called this capacity <strong>spirit</strong>, and through it he defined the human being as the being that can say ‘no’: it can fast when hungry or keep quiet when furious. It is the philosophical version of what Gehlen, from the side of biology, would soon afterwards call a deficient being.</p>\n<p><strong>Unfinished: life as a task.</strong> Saying that human beings are open or unfinished does not mean that they are a blank page. It means that what we are is not entirely settled by our biology, and that part of it we have to make ourselves. José Ortega y Gasset put it in a provocative formula in the essay <em>History as a System</em>: ‘man has no nature; what he has is... history’. He did not deny that we have a body with its own laws; he meant that what is properly human is not given to us as its weight is given to a stone or its ferocity to a tiger, but is made over the course of a life and a history. Life, he says in effect, is given to us, but not ready-made: at every step we have to decide what to do with it. That is why the question ‘what is the human being?’ links up with the one in the last section, ‘what meaning does my life have?’: if we were not unfinished, we would not have to ask it. Something to think about: which part of what you are was given to you, and which part have you made yourself? Can the line be drawn?</p>\n<p><strong>Three ideas that do not fit together.</strong> In 1928, in a short essay published in English as <em>Man’s Place in Nature</em>, the German philosopher <a href=\"#biografias/scheler\">Max Scheler</a> made a diagnosis that still holds. Anyone educated in Europe, he said, carries in their head three images of the human being that come from different traditions: the Judaeo-Christian, which sees humans as God’s creatures, fallen and called to be saved; the Greek, which defines them by reason and places them above the other animals; and that of modern natural science, for which they are a late product of evolution, differing from other animals only in degree. Each has its reasons, but they do not form a whole: we use them according to the moment, without noticing that they contradict one another. That is why, Scheler concluded, so much has never been known about the human being and yet humans have never been so problematic to themselves. From this dissatisfaction philosophical anthropology was born as a discipline in its own right, and from Scheler too comes the idea, which you have just read, that the human being is ‘open to the world’.</p>\n<p>More than two thousand years ago, the Latin comic playwright Terence put into a character’s mouth a line that became the motto of humanism: ‘I am human, and I consider nothing human alien to me.’ Nothing human is alien to us, neither the best nor the worst: the same being that is capable of writing a symphony is capable of torture. Pascal expressed it in the seventeenth century by calling the human being ‘a reed, the weakest in nature, but a thinking reed’. And there is an added difficulty: almost everything we do every day we do on <strong>autopilot</strong>, out of habit, without thinking about it, and when we try to study ourselves we are both the investigator and the investigated. That is why knowing ourselves is as difficult as it is necessary.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\">\n<p>“The human being is the measure of all things: of things that are, that they are, and of things that are not, that they are not.” — Protagoras (quoted by Plato, <em>Theaetetus</em>, 152a, and by Sextus Empiricus)</p> </blockquote>\n<p>Today it is usually read in a humanist key, as if it put the human being at the centre of the cosmos, but that is a modern reading. Protagoras was speaking about truth: there is no absolute criterion beyond what seems so to each person, or to each community. It is a <strong>relativist</strong> thesis, which we will meet again in Topic 3. This topic is, at bottom, an attempt to look at ourselves in the mirror with the critical attention that characterises philosophy.</p>\n<p>Nor has the idea of a common <strong>humanity</strong> always existed. Many peoples have called themselves by a word that means ‘the people’, as if others were less so: that is what <em>inuit</em> means, for example. The Greeks called those who did not speak Greek ‘barbarians’. The Stoics argued that all human beings are citizens of one and the same world, but slavery remained normal for centuries, and as late as 1550, at the Valladolid debate, Bartolomé de las Casas and <a href=\"#biografias/sepulveda\">Juan Ginés de Sepúlveda</a> argued over whether the native peoples of America had the same dignity as Europeans. That today we take a common humanity for granted is the result of a long history, not a starting point.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: choose a work (a painting, a song, a film, a piece of graffiti) that, in your view, shows something characteristic of the human being, and explain in five lines what it shows. Then think about burials: why is treating a corpse with care a clue that someone is asking about life? And about Scheler: of his three images of the human being, which do you hear most around you, at home, in television series or in class?</p>\n<h2>4. Nature or culture? A biological and cultural animal</h2>\n<p><strong>Two ways of putting the question.</strong> When we ask what we are, there are two broad approaches. The first looks for a <strong>natural specificity</strong>: a set of stable traits of our own, tied to our biology, that any human being in any age would have, such as reason, language, morality or the ability to make tools. Aristotle reasoned in this way when he defined the human being as a <em>zôon politikón</em>, an animal made by nature to live in a community (<em>Politics</em>, I, 2). The second stresses <strong>historical and cultural conditions</strong>: what is human is not fixed in advance, but is shaped within a history, an environment and a culture. Marx condensed this in the sixth of his <em>Theses on Feuerbach</em> (1845): the human essence is not an abstraction that each individual carries inside, but the ensemble of social relations. Until well into the 19th century, philosophers such as Kant or Schopenhauer took a universal human nature for granted: institutions and customs changed, the species did not. Darwin, Freud and Nietzsche, each in his own way, brought it into history: what we are by nature also has a history, whether biological, psychological or moral. That is why the question is not settled by choosing a side. The two dimensions influence each other constantly, and this section is about that tension.</p>\n<p><strong>Six positions in two camps.</strong> These two approaches take shape in positions with names of their own. On the side of nature are <strong>evolutionism</strong>, which begins with Darwin; <strong>ethology</strong>, the study of animal behaviour in its natural environment, with which Konrad Lorenz described how goslings follow the first creature they see moving when they hatch (<strong>imprinting</strong>); and Wilson’s <strong>sociobiology</strong>, which you will see a little further on. On the side of culture are <strong>behaviourism</strong>, for which we are what the environment teaches us; <strong>historicism</strong>, which <a href=\"#biografias/ortega\">Ortega y Gasset</a> condensed in <em>History as a System</em> (1935) by saying that the human being has no nature, but a history; and Sartre’s <strong>existentialism</strong>. One warning applies to all of them: that a behaviour has a genetic basis does not make it good. The natural and the right are different things, and leaping from one to the other is the fallacy you will see with social Darwinism.</p>\n<table> <thead> <tr> <th>Position</th> <th>Central idea</th> <th>A name</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td><strong>Evolutionism</strong></td> <td>We are the result of millions of years of evolution; with other genes we would be different beings</td> <td>Charles Darwin (1859)</td> </tr> <tr> <td><strong>Ethology</strong></td> <td>There are instincts: behaviours that are not learnt and that we can restrain, but not erase</td> <td>Konrad Lorenz (Nobel Prize in 1973)</td> </tr> <tr> <td><strong>Sociobiology</strong></td> <td>Many social behaviours (caring for relatives, competing, defending a territory) have a genetic root</td> <td>Edward O. Wilson (1975)</td> </tr> <tr> <td><strong>Behaviourism</strong></td> <td>The mind is born as a blank slate (<em>tabula rasa</em>): everything is learning</td> <td>John B. Watson (1913), B. F. Skinner</td> </tr> <tr> <td><strong>Historicism</strong></td> <td>Each human being is their life, their time and their circumstances</td> <td>José Ortega y Gasset</td> </tr> <tr> <td><strong>Existentialism</strong></td> <td>There is no prior model: each person makes themselves by choosing, and cannot stop choosing</td> <td>Jean-Paul Sartre (1946)</td> </tr> </tbody> </table>\n<p>A first answer to what we are starts from biology. The human being is the fruit of <strong>evolution</strong>, the long process by which species change and transform over time. In the 19th century, the naturalist <strong>Lamarck</strong> was among the first to oppose <em>fixism</em> (the idea that species are fixed and unchanging) and proposed <strong>transformism</strong>: some species descend from others through anatomical changes. According to his <strong>law of use and disuse</strong>, organs that are used develop and those that are not used atrophy; and these <strong>acquired characteristics</strong> are inherited. On this he departs from Darwin. Shortly afterwards, <strong>Charles Darwin</strong> —after observing, among other things, the finches of the Galápagos Islands— and, independently, <strong>Alfred R. Wallace</strong> formulated the great theory: all living beings have a <strong>common origin</strong> and species evolve through <strong>natural selection</strong>. In each generation individuals are born with different characteristics; those best adapted to the environment survive and reproduce more, passing on those traits. Today, combined with genetics and <strong>Mendel</strong>’s laws of heredity, this explanation is known as the <em>synthetic theory</em> or <strong>neo-Darwinism</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>The giraffe, told twice.</strong> Until the 19th century <strong>fixism</strong> prevailed: species were thought to have always existed just as they are, and it was with this idea that Linnaeus classified living things (<em>Systema Naturae</em>, 1735). To see what changed afterwards, a classic example will do: the giraffe’s neck. <strong>Lamarck</strong> (<em>Zoological Philosophy</em>, 1809) explained it like this: short-necked ancestors stretched their necks to reach the high leaves; the effort lengthened them a little over their lifetime, and their young inherited that slightly longer neck. <strong>Darwin</strong> tells the story the other way round. Among the giraffes of any generation there is already <strong>variation</strong>: some are born with a slightly longer neck than others, not because they need it, but by chance. When food is scarce, those with longer necks eat better, survive more and have more young, which inherit the trait. The difference is fundamental. For Lamarck, change begins in the life of the individual and responds to a need; for Darwin, variation comes first, it is blind to what would be useful, and the environment only <em>selects</em> among what is already there. In 1888, August Weismann cut the tails off mice for five generations, and the young were still born with tails: what happens to a body is not written into heredity. One final nuance: some biologists now think that fights between males, who strike each other with their necks, also played a part in the long neck. The example helps us understand the logic; it is not the last word on giraffes.</p>\n<p><strong>What Darwin was missing.</strong> Darwin knew that traits are inherited, but not how. Like many in his day, he believed that the parents’ characteristics blend in their children like two paints, and in that case any novelty would be diluted within a few generations. The answer lay in a piece of work that hardly anyone read: in 1866 the monk Gregor Mendel published his experiments with peas, which showed that heredity is passed on in separate units that do not blend, what we now call <em>genes</em>. Rediscovered in 1900, Mendel ended up joined to Darwin in the <strong>modern synthesis</strong>, which took shape in the 1930s and 1940s and which Julian Huxley named in a book of 1942. Its basic idea: <strong>mutations</strong>, small random changes in genetic material, are the source of new variants; sexual reproduction combines them, and natural selection, together with sheer chance in small populations, decides which of them spread. In 1953 James Watson and Francis Crick, using Rosalind Franklin’s X-ray images, described the structure of DNA. Today the evidence comes from many directions that agree: fossils, comparative anatomy, genetic kinship between species and evolution observed as it happens. That is why the geneticist Theodosius Dobzhansky gave a 1973 essay a title that has become famous: nothing in biology makes sense except in the light of evolution.</p>\n<p><strong>After the synthesis: three revisions.</strong> The synthetic theory did not close the debate. The American biologist <strong>Lynn Margulis</strong> (1938-2011) argued in 1967, after some fifteen journals had rejected her article, that the mitochondria and chloroplasts of cells were originally free-living bacteria that stayed to live inside other cells. For those organelles this is now accepted doctrine; Margulis went further and spoke of <strong>symbiogenesis</strong>: sometimes novelty is born from the union of different organisms, not only from competition between them, a general thesis that is still debated. In 1972, the palaeontologists Niles Eldredge and Stephen Jay Gould proposed <strong>punctuated equilibrium</strong>: species would spend long periods almost without change and would change in brief episodes (brief in geological time, that is, thousands of years), which would explain why transitional fossils are scarce. It is still debated what share of evolution follows this rhythm. And in 1976 Richard Dawkins, in <em>The Selfish Gene</em>, coined the word <strong>meme</strong> for units of culture (a melody, an idea, a fashion) that are copied from brain to brain and compete with one another like genes. Internet memes took their name from this; the analogy is suggestive, although many anthropologists doubt that culture can be divided into units.</p>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t2-76\">Natural selection is easier to understand step by step, with a present-day case: bacteria that resist antibiotics. First, among millions of bacteria there is <strong>variation</strong>: through random <strong>mutations</strong>, a few have a gene that protects them from the drug. Second, those differences are <strong>inherited</strong>: the resistant bacterium passes the gene on to its descendants. Third, the <strong>environment</strong> changes: the patient takes the antibiotic, which kills almost all of them. Fourth, the resistant ones survive and reproduce more, and within a few days almost the whole population is resistant. Nobody designed that change and no bacterium ‘wanted’ to resist: variation, inheritance and an environment that favours some over others are enough. That is why the philosopher Daniel Dennett (<em>Darwin’s Dangerous Idea</em>, 1995) said that, if he had to give an award for the best idea in history, he would give it to Darwin: it explains the order of living things without appealing to any purpose.</p>\n<p>Applied to the human being, evolution explains <strong>hominisation</strong>: the biological process that, over millions of years, led from the first primates to <em>Homo sapiens</em>. A frequent misunderstanding should be cleared up: we do not descend from the chimpanzee, but share a <strong>common ancestor</strong> with it. Among the decisive changes of hominisation are <strong>bipedal walking</strong> (walking upright freed the hands and transformed the spine), <strong>encephalisation</strong> (the increase in the size and connections of the brain), the <strong>freeing of the hands</strong> with an <strong>opposable thumb</strong> that made it possible to make tools, and the development of a complex <strong>social life</strong>.</p>\n<p>The philosopher José Ortega y Gasset devoted a short and very influential book to technology, <em>Meditation on Technique</em> (1939). Its starting point is simple and surprising: human beings are not content merely to <strong>be</strong> in the world, to survive; what they truly seek is to <strong>be well</strong>. An animal is satisfied with covering its biological needs; we, by contrast, call ‘necessary’ everything that allows us to live as we believe life is worth living. That is why <strong>well-being</strong> is not a fixed measure: it changes from one era to another and from one society to another.</p><p>Hence his definition: <strong>technology</strong> is the reform that human beings impose on nature in order to satisfy their needs. Because those needs change, technology is not a closed repertoire of tools, but a reality always in movement. And behind it there is something deeper: for Ortega, each person is a <strong>vital programme</strong>, a life project that is not yet made and that has to be realised. Technology is the means to carry out that programme in a world that does not give it to us already solved.</p><p>Think of a student in 1900: he studied by the light of a candle or an oil lamp and wrote letters that took days to arrive. For him, ‘being well’ meant having good light and a punctual post. Today, being well includes being connected almost always. But every advance brings new needs that did not exist before: chargers, spare batteries, secure passwords, antivirus software, protection against data theft. Technology solves one problem and, in doing so, creates others.</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: do the needs we create with technology make us live better or merely live differently? What ‘need’ of yours did not exist thirty years ago?</p>\n<p>Darwin changed our image of ourselves in three ways. We are <strong>relatives</strong> of all other living things: we share almost 99% of our DNA with the chimpanzee and, with the mouse, about 85% of the sequences of the genes that make proteins. Evolution <strong>has no goal</strong>: it is not a ladder climbing towards the human being but a branching tree, and we are just one more branch. And the appearance of design does not need a designer: in 1802 William Paley had argued that, if we find a watch in a field, we think of a watchmaker, and that the eye demands a creator even more; natural selection explains the eye without one. At the end of the twentieth century, the <strong>intelligent design</strong> movement again argued that certain organs are too complex to have evolved, but it does not put forward evidence that can be tested, and in 2005 a court in Pennsylvania (Kitzmiller v. Dover) concluded that it is not science. Faith is another matter: the Catholic Church, for example, considers evolution compatible with belief in God, and in 1996 John Paul II acknowledged it as ‘more than a hypothesis’. You will see this in the section on religion in the <a href=\"#teoria/fil-metafisica\">topic on reality</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>Three things evolution does not say.</strong> Darwin’s theory is one of the most quoted and one of the most misunderstood. First confusion: thinking that to evolve is to <strong>progress</strong>. Evolution does not go from worse to better, but from what fitted one environment to what fits another. The Mexican blind cavefish, which lives in caves without light, has lost its eyes: that is evolution, even though nobody would call it progress. It is no accident that Darwin preferred to speak of “descent with modification”. Second: thinking that it has a <strong>purpose</strong>. Saying that the giraffe’s neck “grew so that it could reach the leaves” is convenient but misleading: nothing in the process aims at a goal, and no species “tries” to adapt. Third: thinking that <strong>the strongest</strong> wins. The phrase “survival of the fittest” is not Darwin’s but the philosopher Herbert Spencer’s (<em>The Principles of Biology</em>, 1864), although Darwin later adopted it. And “fit” does not mean strong, aggressive or clever, but suited to an environment, and in biology it is measured by something very prosaic: how many descendants are left. A bacterium, an ant cooperating with thousands of sisters or an orchid that tricks an insect can all be very fit. One last warning: it is not individuals that evolve, but populations. No giraffe changed during its lifetime.</p>\n<p><strong>Social Darwinism.</strong> At the end of the 19th century some authors carried the struggle for existence over from nature to society. The most influential was <strong>Herbert Spencer</strong> (1820-1903): competition between individuals, companies and peoples would weed out the worst and move humanity forward, and so the state should not help the poor, because it would be protecting the “unfit”. This position is called <strong>Social Darwinism</strong>, even though Darwin did not defend it. From the same period comes <strong>eugenics</strong>, a word coined in 1883 by Francis Galton, Darwin’s cousin, for a programme that set out to “improve” the species by deciding who should have children. Its effects were real: forced sterilisation laws in the United States, which the Supreme Court upheld in 1927, or in Sweden, in force until 1976, and, in its most extreme form, the Nazi law of 1933 and the crimes that followed. Even then there were replies from biology itself: the Russian geographer Peter Kropotkin (<em>Mutual Aid</em>, 1902) gathered observations of animals and peoples to show that cooperation matters in evolution as much as competition. What matters is to see that Social Darwinism does not apply biology but makes ideological use of it: it takes a theory that describes how species change and turns it into a rule about how we ought to treat one another.</p>\n<p><strong>Why it is a fallacy.</strong> Social Darwinism fails on two counts. The first is an error of fact: it confuses biological success, measured in descendants, with social success, measured in money or power, and it forgets that in a species as social as ours cooperation has been decisive. The second is an error of logic, and it weighs more: how things <em>are</em> does not tell us how they <em>ought</em> to be. That the weak die earlier in nature does not prove that we should let them die, just as the existence of diseases does not prove that we should not cure them. Hume had already noted that many arguments slip without warning from “is” to “ought” (<em>A Treatise of Human Nature</em>, III, 1, 1), and G. E. Moore called the attempt to define the good by a natural property the <strong>naturalistic fallacy</strong>; you will see it in Topic 5. Thomas H. Huxley, Darwin’s great defender, drew the opposite conclusion to Spencer’s in <em>Evolution and Ethics</em> (1893): moral progress does not consist in imitating the natural struggle, but in putting a brake on it. The fallacy also has a friendly version, straight out of the adverts: “it’s natural, so it’s good”. Something to think about: what examples of this argument can you find in advertising or on social media? Is hemlock good, since it is natural too?</p>\n<p>Three qualifications about hominisation. A bigger brain is not, simply, a more intelligent one: Neanderthals had, on average, a slightly larger brain than ours; how it is organised also matters. The hands were freed <strong>before</strong> there were elaborate tools: Lucy, the famous <em>Australopithecus afarensis</em> from about 3.2 million years ago, already walked upright with a brain like a chimpanzee’s. And walking on two legs came at a price: the pelvis narrowed while the head grew, which is why human childbirth is difficult and babies are born very immature. This is the biological basis of Portmann’s ‘social womb’.</p>\n<p><strong>Why walk on two feet?</strong> <strong>Bipedalism</strong> had a cost, as you have just seen, so it had to bring compensation. There are three main hypotheses, and they are not mutually exclusive. It frees the hands to carry food, infants or tools. It saves energy on long journeys: a 2007 study (Sockol, Raichlen and Pontzer) measured that a human walking spends about a quarter of what a chimpanzee spends covering the same distance. And it exposes less of the body’s surface to the midday sun and more to the breeze, an advantage in open, hot environments. The old idea that we stood upright to see above the savannah grass has lost ground, because the first bipeds lived in environments with trees. What is certain is the result: free hands that the brain learnt to exploit, and a brain that grew at the same time as the tools those hands made.</p>\n<p><strong>From the obstetric dilemma to solidarity.</strong> Anthropologists call the clash between two trends in evolution the <strong>obstetric dilemma</strong>: walking on two legs narrowed the pelvis, while the brain kept on growing. The way out was a compromise: we are born early, with the brain only half formed, which is why a human infant depends on others for years. That long dependence meant that care could not be the job of a single mother but of the whole group, and it pushed towards complex social structures. This biological fact has a philosophical reading. For Hannah Arendt (<em>The Human Condition</em>, 1958), <strong>natality</strong>, the fact that each person comes into the world as a new beginning, is the root of the human capacity to act and to start something. For Emmanuel Levinas (<em>Totality and Infinity</em>, 1961), ethics arises precisely in the face of the other's fragility, which calls on us to answer for them. So our original <strong>vulnerability</strong> is not just a problem that evolution solved as best it could: it is one of the roots of social and moral life.</p>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t2-11\">But biology is not enough. On top of hominisation there is built <strong>humanisation</strong>: the process by which that hominid becomes fully human thanks to <strong>culture</strong>. Four great factors made it possible: mastery of <strong>fire</strong>, the making of <strong>tools</strong>, the appearance of <strong>agriculture and livestock farming</strong> (which allowed settled life) and <strong>social organisation</strong>. <strong>Language</strong> is the hinge that joins both processes, because it makes it possible to pass what has been learned from one generation to the next.</p>\n<p>If hominisation is measured in bones and tools, humanisation is recognised by five <strong>features</strong>, which no fossil shows in full:</p><ul><li><strong>Creating and passing on culture.</strong> We do not just adapt to our surroundings: we transform them, and hand customs, techniques and beliefs on to the next generation.</li><li><strong>Living in a world of symbols.</strong> We give things meanings that go beyond what they are: a language, a rite, a flag or a law.</li><li><strong>Complex social relationships.</strong> Nobody becomes human alone: we come to know ourselves through others, and identity is formed within a community.</li><li><strong>Morality.</strong> Human groups regulate behaviour and judge what is right and what is wrong; our conduct is not merely instinctive, but guided by values.</li><li><strong>Openness to transcendence.</strong> We know that we will die, and we ask about causes and ultimate meaning. From that question come religions, philosophy and the deepest function of art.</li></ul>\n<p>In <em>Meditation on Technique</em>, Ortega does not recount the history of technology through its inventions, but through the relationship that human beings have with it. He thus distinguishes three great stages.</p><ol><li><strong>Technology of chance.</strong> This is that of prehistory. Resources are few and simple, and the whole group knows them, so there are no specialists. No one is conscious of ‘doing technology’: discoveries, such as lighting a fire by rubbing two pieces of wood together, appear by chance and spread among everyone as just another custom.</li><li><strong>Technology of the craftsman.</strong> This is that of Greece, Rome and the Middle Ages. Tools multiply and trades appear: the blacksmith, the carpenter, the shoemaker. Knowledge is passed on from master to apprentice in the workshop, through practice. The craftsman knows he has a skill, but sees it as his trade, not as ‘technology’ in general; and he remains the centre of the process: the tool merely prolongs his hand.</li><li><strong>Technology of the technician.</strong> It begins with the Industrial Revolution. Now the protagonist is the <strong>machine</strong>, which works by itself. Work is divided between the engineers who design it and the operatives who watch over it or operate it.</li></ol><p>An example: obtaining bread. In the first stage, someone discovers by chance that some crushed grains placed next to the fire become edible. In the second, a baker kneads, controls the yeast and decides when to take the loaf out of the oven; he knows how to do everything. In the third, an automated industrial plant produces thousands of loaves per hour, and the person who supervises it has perhaps never kneaded dough.</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: what is gained and what is lost when the operative no longer masters the whole process? Does something similar happen to you with the mobile phone you use every day?</p>\n<p><strong>Are we human yet?</strong> The Catalan palaeontologist <strong>Eudald Carbonell</strong>, one of the co-directors of the Atapuerca sites, defends with Robert Sala in <em>We Are Not Yet Human</em> (2002) a provocative thesis: humanisation has not ended. We have acquired a technology capable of transforming the planet, but not a consciousness equal to it; we still think like tribes when our problems (the climate, pandemics, weapons) already concern the whole species. For Carbonell, we will be fully human when we socialise knowledge and think as a species, so that a dignified life reaches everyone. It can be objected that he mixes a description, what we are, with an ideal, what we ought to be. But the objection also shows the strength of his thesis: ‘human’ is not merely a biological label, but a task.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Species</th><th>Age (approx.)</th><th>Key feature</th><th>Cranial capacity</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><em>Sahelanthropus tchadensis</em></td><td>~7 Ma</td><td>Possibly the first hominid</td><td>~350 cc</td></tr>\n<tr><td><em>Australopithecus afarensis</em> (“Lucy”)</td><td>3.9–2.9 Ma</td><td>Clear bipedalism</td><td>400–500 cc</td></tr>\n<tr><td><em>Homo habilis</em></td><td>2.4–1.6 Ma</td><td>First stone tools</td><td>600–700 cc</td></tr>\n<tr><td><em>Homo erectus</em></td><td>1.9 Ma–110,000 yrs</td><td>Leaves Africa; masters fire</td><td>900–1,100 cc</td></tr>\n<tr><td><em>Homo neanderthalensis</em></td><td>400,000–40,000 yrs</td><td>Europe and Asia; burials</td><td>~1,500 cc</td></tr>\n<tr><td><em>Homo sapiens</em></td><td>300,000 yrs–present</td><td>Language, art, culture</td><td>~1,350 cc</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The key is that we are born biologically “unfinished”: the German philosopher and anthropologist <strong>Arnold Gehlen</strong> called the human being a <strong>deficient being</strong> (<em>Mängelwesen</em>). Unlike other animals, which come into the world with <strong>instincts</strong> that tell them how to act and live in a <strong>closed world</strong>, the human being has very reduced instincts and inhabits an <strong>open world</strong> that it must build. That is why the Swiss biologist Adolf Portmann spoke of a “social womb”: culture is a kind of “second matrix” in which we finish being formed. Thus, nature and culture are not opposed, but need each other: we are at once a product of biology and of culture. This relationship is called the <strong>nature–culture dialectic</strong>, and it lies at the heart of an old debate: the one that sets <strong>innatism</strong> (what is decisive is what we bring “from the factory”, what is inherited) against <strong>environmentalism</strong> (what is decisive is what we learn from our surroundings). The most reasonable answer does not choose an extreme: we are both, intertwined.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr> <th></th> <th><strong>Nature</strong> (the biological dimension)</th> <th><strong>Culture</strong> (the learnt dimension)</th> </tr> </thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr> <td>What does it provide?</td> <td>The organism, the brain, attenuated instincts and evolutionarily transmitted capacities</td> <td>The knowledge, values, language, techniques and customs characteristic of a society</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td>How is it acquired?</td> <td>Through genetic inheritance: we are born with it</td> <td>Through learning and <strong>socialisation</strong>: it is acquired by living together in society</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td>Process involved</td> <td><strong>Hominisation</strong></td> <td><strong>Humanisation</strong></td> </tr>\n<tr> <td>Is it identical everywhere?</td> <td>Yes: it is shared by the whole species</td> <td>No: it varies according to the society and the era</td> </tr>\n<tr> <td>Example</td> <td>Coming into the world with the faculty of speech</td> <td>Acquiring a particular language (Basque, Spanish…)</td> </tr> </tbody> </table>\n<p><strong>The wild boy of Aveyron, with caution.</strong> If culture is a second womb, what happens to someone who grows up outside it? Many cases of <strong>feral children</strong> have been reported, supposedly raised alone or among animals, but almost all are poorly documented, and some, such as the “wolf girls” of India in the 1920s, raise serious doubts. The best studied is that of <strong>Victor of Aveyron</strong>, a boy of about twelve caught in the woods of southern France at the beginning of 1800. He did not speak, did not answer to his name and seemed indifferent to the cold. The young doctor Jean Itard took charge of him in Paris, tried to educate him for years and described it in two reports, of 1801 and 1806. Victor learnt to recognise objects, to show affection and to read a few words, but he never managed to speak. He died in 1828, and François Truffaut brought his story to the cinema in <em>The Wild Child</em> (1970). What does the case prove? Less than it seems. It is tempting to conclude that without culture there is no full humanity, or that language can only be learnt before a certain age; but we do not know how long Victor lived alone, nor whether he was abandoned precisely because he already had a disability. A single case, and an uncertain one at that, suggests a hypothesis; it does not prove it.</p>\n<p>How far does <strong>nature</strong> reach in what we do? In 1975, the biologist <strong><a href=\"#biografias/wilson\">Edward O. Wilson</a></strong> (1929-2021) proposed <strong>sociobiology</strong>: studying the social behaviour of animals — and of humans too — as a product of <strong>evolution</strong>. Many behaviours we think purely cultural (cooperation, the care of children, a certain sense of fairness) would have, he argued, a <strong>biological root</strong> selected because it helped survival. The idea set off a fierce controversy: its critics feared that “the natural” would be used to present inequalities as inevitable. Wilson himself stressed that, in species like ours, evolutionary success owes more to <strong>cooperation</strong> than to struggle.</p>\n<p><strong>How much do genes weigh?</strong> To separate what is inherited from what is learnt, science makes use of an experiment that nature carries out on its own: <strong>twins</strong>. Identical twins share all their genes; non-identical twins share half, like any other pair of siblings. If, for a given trait, identical twins are much more alike than non-identical ones, that trait has an important genetic basis. Even more revealing are identical twins brought up in different families, like those studied from 1979 by Thomas Bouchard’s team at the University of Minnesota: they were alike in height, in personality traits and sometimes in surprising tastes. From this comes a figure, <strong>heritability</strong>, which has to be read carefully. It does not say what part of me comes from my genes, but what part of the <em>differences</em> between the people of a particular population is due to genetic differences. And it can be high without the environment ceasing to matter: height is highly heritable, and yet South Korean women born in 1996 are on average about twenty centimetres taller than those born in 1896, with no genetic change at all, thanks to diet and healthcare. Moreover, the brain is <strong>plastic</strong>: its connections form and strengthen with use, above all in childhood and adolescence. Nor is a gene a destiny. Phenylketonuria is a genetic disease that seriously damages the brain, but if it is detected with the heel-prick test given to newborn babies and a suitable diet is followed, the child grows up healthy.</p>\n<p><strong>Neither determinism nor a blank slate.</strong> The underlying debate is still alive, and it is worth knowing the positions with their best arguments. In <em>The Blank Slate</em> (2002), the psychologist Steven Pinker argues that much of 20th-century social science rested on three ideas that no longer hold: that the mind is born empty, like a blank page written on only by experience (the image is associated with Locke); that human beings are good by nature and society corrupts them (the “noble savage”, attributed to Rousseau); and that a soul makes decisions apart from the body (the “ghost in the machine”, in Ryle’s phrase). For Pinker, recognising a human nature does not force us to accept inequalities: the fact that people are different does not stop them having the same rights. His critics fear precisely the opposite. As early as 1984 the geneticist Richard Lewontin, the neurobiologist Steven Rose and the psychologist Leon Kamin had warned in <em>Not in Our Genes</em> that <strong>biological determinism</strong> has often been used to present as natural injustices that were social, and that genetic explanations of behaviour tend to go further than the data allow. Between the two extremes, many researchers now accept the idea that the science writer Matt Ridley summed up in the title of a 2003 book, <em>Nature via Nurture</em>: nature <em>through</em> nurture, not nature <em>against</em> nurture. How much each side weighs in each trait is an empirical question, but one with political consequences, and that is why it is argued over so passionately.</p>\n<p>Culture is, moreover, a much faster way of adapting than biology. It takes a species thousands of generations to develop a thicker coat; a human group only has to learn from another how to sew clothes from skins. The anthropologists Robert Boyd and Peter Richerson (<em>Not by Genes Alone</em>, 2005) explain in this way why human beings live in the Arctic and in the desert with hardly any change to their bodies. But this adaptation has limits: a tradition can also prevent change. The Norse settlers who lived in Greenland between the tenth and fifteenth centuries went on raising livestock as in Scandinavia and did not adopt the hunting techniques of their Inuit neighbours; when the climate cooled, their settlements disappeared. Cultures change on their own, through inventions, borrowings and contact with others, and not always in the most convenient direction.</p>\n<p><strong>Do other animals have culture?</strong> It depends on what we mean by culture. If it is behaviour learnt from others and not carried in the genes, the answer seems to be yes. The most famous case is that of the macaques on the Japanese island of <strong>Koshima</strong>. In 1953 a young female whom the researchers called Imo began to wash in the water the sweet potatoes that they themselves left on the beach. The habit passed first to her playmates and her mother, then to other young monkeys and females; the adult males were the slowest. Two nuances are needed. The behaviour arose because humans were feeding the monkeys, so it is not an entirely natural case. And the legend of the “hundredth monkey”, according to which the habit suddenly jumped to other islands when the hundredth monkey learnt it, is an invention spread by esoteric books in 1979 and 1981, with no basis in the data. With chimpanzees the evidence is broader: in 1999 a team led by Andrew Whiten compared several communities studied for decades and found 39 behaviours, such as cracking nuts with stones or fishing for termites with twigs, that vary from one group to another for no ecological reason. Where, then, does the distinctiveness of human culture lie? For the psychologist Michael Tomasello (<em>The Cultural Origins of Human Cognition</em>, 1999), in <strong>accumulation</strong>: we keep each improvement and use it as the starting point for the next, like a ratchet that will not let us slip back. Nobody invented a mobile phone on their own.</p>\n<p><strong>Close cousins.</strong> We share almost 99% of our DNA with the chimpanzee, and some apes have learnt hundreds of signs. Besides Washoe, whom you will meet in section 6, the most famous case is that of the gorilla <strong>Koko</strong>, whom the psychologist Francine Patterson taught from 1972 a version of American Sign Language; according to her carer, she came to use about a thousand signs. Critics objected that complete data were never published and that the carers interpreted the sequences of signs generously. Does what unites us weigh more than what separates us? That one per cent of difference is small in the genome and enormous in its effects: no ape has written a book, taught another to count or asked what an ape is.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: does a behaviour having biological roots mean we cannot change it, or only that it costs us more? Where does “nature” end and “culture” begin, if there is a clear border at all? You will study Wilson in Year 2; here it is enough to keep in mind that the question “nature or culture?” is also debated from biology.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: (1) Place the six positions in the table on a line running from ‘everything is nature’ to ‘everything is culture’ and explain each one in a sentence of your own. (2) Imagine a day in the life of a hominid from two million years ago and recount it in ten lines, with at least three changes of hominisation and one of humanisation. (3) Place bets in class on which feature of the body was most decisive for survival; then look it up and check who was right. (4) Do you agree with Carbonell? What would have to happen for us to say that humanity ‘has finished’ becoming human?</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Debate: what is love? For Wilson’s sociobiology (<em>On Human Nature</em>, 1978), attachment and love are, at bottom, mechanisms that natural selection has favoured because they help to raise children. The Chilean biologist Humberto Maturana maintained the opposite as regards the order: what made us human was love, which he understood as accepting the other as a legitimate other in coexistence, and language and cooperation were built upon it afterwards. Divide the class into two groups; each prepares three arguments and an example. At the end, write together a conclusion that gathers the best of both positions. Are they truly incompatible?</p>\n<p>To go further: the reading <a href=\"#lecturas/fil-lec-naturaleza-cultura\">‘Nature and culture: Mosterín, Cavalli-Sforza and Sloterdijk’</a> brings together four documents by three authors on where what is inherited ends and what is learnt begins. Mosterín understands culture as information that travels not in the genes but from one person to another; Cavalli-Sforza compares the change of cultures with the evolution of species, and Sloterdijk asks what will happen when technology allows us to modify our biological inheritance as well.</p>\n<h2>5. Culture, identity and diversity</h2>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t2-18\">What exactly do we mean by <strong>culture</strong>? In the philosophical and anthropological sense it does not refer only to opera or museums, but to something much broader. The anthropologist <strong>E. B. Tylor</strong> already defined it in the 19th century as the set of knowledge, beliefs, art, morals, law, customs and habits that a person acquires as a member of a society. Curiously, the word <em>culture</em> comes from the Latin <em>colere</em>, “to cultivate” (the land), whereas <em>nature</em> comes from <em>nascere</em>, “to be born”: what is born by itself as opposed to what has to be cultivated with effort.</p>\n<p><strong>Three ways of looking at culture.</strong> Tylor’s definition appears in <em>Primitive Culture</em> (1871), and the title already says a great deal about its time. Tylor and other anthropologists of the period, such as the American Lewis H. Morgan (<em>Ancient Society</em>, 1877), were <strong>evolutionists</strong>: they believed that all societies pass through the same stages, from “savagery” to “barbarism” and from there to “civilisation”, and that industrial Europe was in the lead. With this scheme, differences between cultures were read as differences in time, as if other peoples were a past still alive. It did not help that their data were often second-hand: accounts by travellers, missionaries and traders. At the beginning of the 20th century <strong>Franz Boas</strong> rejected that ladder: each culture has its own history and must be studied in the field, in its own language and on its own terms, before being compared; this is the origin of the methodological relativism you will see further on. Later, <strong>Clifford Geertz</strong> (<em>The Interpretation of Cultures</em>, 1973) proposed seeing culture as a web of meanings that human beings themselves have spun and in which they live suspended. The anthropologist, then, does not measure stages: he interprets, like someone reading a text. A knowing wink and a twitch of the eyelid are the same movement; only culture tells us which is which.</p>\n<p><strong>What can be touched and what cannot.</strong> It is usual to distinguish between <strong>material culture</strong>, the objects a society makes and uses (tools, houses, clothes, a txalaparta, a mobile phone), and <strong>intangible culture</strong>, what cannot be touched: language, beliefs, norms, knowledge, festivals, music or the art of improvising verse. The boundary is porous, because every object is worth what it means: a makila or a team shirt is just wood or cloth, but it matters for what it represents. Since 2003 a UNESCO convention has also protected intangible cultural heritage, which is lost more easily than a building: it is enough for it to stop being handed down. Whatever form it takes, culture has some common features:</p><ul><li><strong>It is learnt and passed on:</strong> it is not in the genes; it passes from some people to others and from one generation to the next.</li><li><strong>It is symbolic:</strong> it works with signs whose meaning is conventional, such as words, rites or laws.</li><li><strong>It forms a system:</strong> its parts are connected; if the economy changes, the family, timetables and even festivals change too.</li><li><strong>It changes and creates:</strong> it is not static; it reinvents itself according to historical and social circumstances.</li><li><strong>It gives identity:</strong> people and groups understand themselves and the world through it.</li></ul>\n<p>Speaking of ‘the’ culture of a people invites two mistakes. One is to imagine it as <strong>closed</strong>: the potato omelette would not exist without an American tuber, and Basque culture extends on both sides of the Bidasoa, between two States. The other is to imagine it as <strong>uniform</strong>: within it there are differences of age, class and origin, and groups which, while sharing the essentials, create their own tastes, slang and rituals, the <strong>subcultures</strong> (the surfers on the coast, video game fans). Others reject the dominant values: these are <strong>countercultures</strong>, such as punk, which in the Basque Country of the eighties inspired Basque radical rock. Beneath this variety there are <strong>cultural universals</strong>, present in all known societies: a language, kinship rules, rituals for saying farewell to the dead and some form of art.</p>\n<p><strong>The same problems, many solutions.</strong> Beneath cultural universals there is something deeper: every human group has to solve similar problems. Who decides for the group; how conflicts are settled; how food is produced and shared out; how territory is occupied; how couples and child-rearing are organised; how the world is explained and values are handed on. What changes are the answers. Among the !Kung of the Kalahari Desert, leadership is informal and is won through speech and prestige, not force. In the Trobriand Islands, studied by Bronisław Malinowski (<em>Argonauts of the Western Pacific</em>, 1922), a ceremonial exchange of gifts, the <em>kula</em>, keeps up friendship between distant islands. Among the Inuit, what is hunted is shared with the whole community, especially in winter. And among the Mosuo, in south-west China, the family is organised around women and couples do not live together: the man visits the woman at night and goes back to his mother’s house. Modern societies have not left these problems behind: they have entrusted them to formal institutions such as parliaments, courts, markets or schools. Some anthropologists, such as Marshall Sahlins or James C. Scott, add an uncomfortable idea: in their centralised power, their hierarchy and their bureaucracy, our societies resemble the first civilisations, such as Mesopotamia or Egypt, more than the small groups of hunter-gatherers.</p>\n<p><strong>Every society rewards a particular way of being.</strong> The American anthropologist <strong>Ruth Benedict</strong>, a pupil of Boas, compared several peoples in <em>Patterns of Culture</em> (1934). Among the Zuñi of New Mexico, the moderate person was esteemed: someone who avoids standing out and distrusts excess. Among the Kwakiutl of the Canadian Pacific coast, by contrast, rivalry and ostentatious expenditure were admired, as in the <em>potlatch</em> feasts, where a chief gained prestige by giving away or destroying his possessions. Benedict borrowed from Nietzsche the adjectives ‘Apollonian’ and ‘Dionysian’ to describe them. Her thesis was that each culture chooses a few traits from the broad range of human possibilities, rewards them and marginalises anyone who does not fit; what is a virtue in one people is an oddity in another. Her colleague <strong>Margaret Mead</strong> took the idea into the realm of the sexes in <em>Sex and Temperament</em> (1935): among the Arapesh of New Guinea, men and women were equally peaceful and caring; among the Mundugumor, equally aggressive; among the Tchambuli, the women ran the trade while the men devoted themselves to adornment and art. Some of those data were later disputed, and Benedict oversimplified complex cultures; but the lesson stood: much of what we call ‘character’ or ‘what is proper to each sex’ is shaped by culture.</p>\n<p><strong>Sex and gender.</strong> From this arises a distinction that is now used everywhere. <strong>Sex</strong> refers to the biological differences of the body; <strong>gender</strong> to what a society expects of someone according to their sex: how to dress, what jobs to do, what emotions to show. The term was borrowed from grammar; it was used in this sense by the psychologist John Money in the 1950s and, in 1972, by the British sociologist Ann Oakley (<em>Sex, Gender and Society</em>), although the underlying idea was already present in <a href=\"#biografias/beauvoir\">Simone de Beauvoir</a>, whom you met in section 1: being a woman is not something given at birth, but something learnt within a society. Sex is biological; gender roles change from one age and place to another. Today there is debate about exactly where the boundary lies: some stress that the body sets limits which culture does not erase; others, such as the philosopher Judith Butler (<em>Gender Trouble</em>, 1990), argue that even our way of understanding sex is shaped by culture. It is an open debate, and it is worth knowing the reasons on each side.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Dilemma: is it worth being ‘normal’? Every society, as Benedict showed, has an idea of what is normal: those who fit in live undisturbed; those who do not receive stares, jokes or rejection. Is it better to adapt, or to be yourself even if it costs you? Is anyone completely ‘normal’? What way of being does your school reward, and whom does it leave out? What would have to change so that nobody had to hide who they are?</p>\n<p>That is why the same person takes part at once in cultures at several <strong>levels</strong>. There is a <strong>global</strong> culture, that of the internet, football or the series watched across half the world; there are <strong>generational</strong> cultures, such as that of people who grew up with a mobile phone in their hand; and there are cultures that cross borders, such as Basque culture, which is lived on both sides of the Bidasoa, in France and in Spain, with a shared language and customs and two different States. Where the ‘national’ level is placed also depends on political perspective: for some, Basque culture is a national culture; for others, a regional culture within wider national cultures. It is an open debate, and it is worth noticing that the label changes depending on who applies it, while the language, the festivals and the cooking remain the same.</p>\n<p>Culture is not inherited through genes: it is transmitted through <strong>socialisation</strong>, the process by which we learn the norms, values and models of our community. It begins in the family and continues through agents such as school, the group of friends, the media or language. Thanks to it we become members of a group and form our <strong>identity</strong>, which has two sides: <strong>personal identity</strong> (what makes me myself and different from others) and <strong>collective identity</strong> (what I share with those who belong to my group, people or culture). Culture, curiously, makes us at once alike (it binds us to a group) and different (each person interprets it in their own way and can even criticise it).</p>\n<p><strong>Enculturation: culture that becomes nature.</strong> Anthropologists call <strong>enculturation</strong> (the term was popularised by Melville Herskovits in 1948) the process by which each person acquires the culture of their own group; it is distinguished from <strong>acculturation</strong>, the change that takes place when two cultures come into contact. Enculturation has a peculiarity: almost all of it happens without our noticing, and so what has been learnt ends up seeming natural. Nobody remembers learning how close to stand when talking to a stranger, to feel disgust at foods that elsewhere are a delicacy, or at what time “people eat”. We experience it as if it came as standard. Aristotle had already observed this: what is habitual becomes like what is natural, because habit resembles nature (<em>Rhetoric</em>, I, 11, 1370a). Cicero gave it the name that has come down to us: through habit a kind of <strong>second nature</strong> is formed (<em>On Ends</em>, V, 74). The expression is apt in a double sense. On the one hand, it explains the strength of culture: changing a habit is almost as hard as changing the body. On the other, it reminds us that it is not the first nature: what has been learnt can, with effort, be examined and learnt differently. That is where a critical look at one’s own culture begins.</p>\n<p>To understand the human being as a living being, it is useful to distinguish two histories that are told on very different scales. These are terms from evolutionary biology, spread in the nineteenth century by the German naturalist Ernst Haeckel.</p><ul><li><strong>Ontogeny</strong>: the development and change of <em>each individual</em> from its origin to its death. Yours began as a single cell, continued with the embryo and the foetus, birth, infancy and the adolescence you are in, and will continue with adulthood and old age.</li><li><strong>Phylogeny</strong>: the change of <em>species</em> over very many generations, forming lineages, like the branches of a tree. The appearance of the first hominids millions of years ago and the lineage that ends in <em>Homo sapiens</em> belong to this history.</li></ul><p>The difference is one of scale: ontogeny is measured in years and happens to an organism; phylogeny is measured in thousands or millions of years and happens to a population. An individual develops, but does not evolve; the species evolves.</p><p>The two histories, however, are connected. Your personal development follows a plan that your species has inherited: that is why you learn to walk upright around your first year of life or begin to speak without anyone teaching you step by step. Haeckel went so far as to claim that the embryo ‘repeats’ the evolution of its ancestors, but that law, taken literally, is not accepted today: the history of the species conditions the development of the individual, it does not copy it. And in the human case a third history must be added, the cultural one: what you learn depends on the family and society in which you grow up.</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: which of your traits come from your individual history and which from that of your species? Is there any about which you cannot decide?</p>\n<p>Our first culture <strong>is not chosen</strong>: nobody decides which language they will learn to speak or which festivals they will celebrate as a child. Later on, though, we can revise it, criticise it or adopt others. Socialisation works through several mechanisms at once: <strong>imitation</strong> of what we see others do (in a 1961 experiment, Albert Bandura showed that children who watched an adult hit a doll then hit it themselves), <strong>reward and punishment</strong>, <strong>identification</strong> with the people we admire, and <strong>language</strong>, which hands us ready-made the categories we think with.</p>\n<p>Learning to live with others <strong>lasts a whole lifetime</strong>. The first stage, <strong>primary socialisation</strong>, takes place in childhood and above all at home: there we learn to talk, to greet people and to tell what is allowed from what is forbidden. Then comes <strong>secondary socialisation</strong>: school, the media, social networks and, later, work. In adolescence the <strong>peer group</strong>, the cuadrilla or group of friends, carries enormous weight, and its opinion on clothes or music can count for more than the family’s; one tries out who one wants to be, sometimes against what one has seen at home. When life takes a sudden turn (retiring, going blind as an adult, entering a monastery), one has to unlearn habits and acquire others: this is <strong>resocialisation</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Relationships with someone who does not know you exist.</strong> In 1956, the sociologists Donald Horton and Richard Wohl used the term <strong>parasocial relationship</strong> for a one-sided bond: the viewer feels that they know the television presenter, rejoices in their successes and misses them on holiday, while the presenter does not know that this viewer exists. With social media the phenomenon has grown: a <em>streamer</em> who greets their audience by name or an <em>influencer</em> who shows off their kitchen creates a sense of closeness that is real for one side and statistical for the other. There is now a further step: companion apps based on artificial intelligence that always reply, remember what you told them and never get tired; in 2018, a Japanese man even held a ‘wedding’ with a virtual character. These relationships are not bad in themselves: they can offer company or serve as a model. The risk appears when they replace reciprocal relationships, which are the ones that truly socialise us, because in them the other person also makes demands on us, contradicts us and changes us.</p>\n<p>Culture is lived within a <strong>society</strong>, and society has something strange about it: nobody invented it and yet it binds us. Émile Durkheim (<em>The Rules of Sociological Method</em>, 1895) called <strong>social facts</strong> those ways of acting that exist before us and are imposed on us: the language we learn, the currency we pay with, the time at which we have lunch. Max Weber warned of the opposite risk: speaking of ‘society’ as if it were someone who makes decisions. For him, only people act, and sociology must explain social phenomena on the basis of what individuals do and the meaning they give to it (<strong>methodological individualism</strong>). Between the two lie <strong>institutions</strong>: a school goes on existing even if all its teachers and students change, because what lasts are the positions (head teacher, form tutors, school council) and the rules that define them.</p>\n<p>The philosopher John Searle (<em>The Construction of Social Reality</em>, 1995) asked how such things exist. A ten-euro note is, physically, a piece of paper; it is money because we all accept that it <em>counts as</em> money. He called these <strong>institutional facts</strong>: they exist because we recognise them collectively, like money, marriage, a border or the Bachillerato certificate. Their power is enormous, but so is their fragility: if people stop accepting them, they disappear. In Germany in 1923, inflation made the mark lose almost all its value in a few months, and some people papered their walls with banknotes because they cost less than wallpaper.</p>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t2-22\">Since cultures are very diverse, different <strong>attitudes</strong> are possible in the face of that diversity, and it is worth telling them apart clearly:</p>\n<ul> <li><strong>Ethnocentrism</strong>: it consists in judging other cultures from one’s own, taking it as the only valid and superior model. It is the root of many prejudices, of racism and of xenophobia.</li> <li><strong>Cultural relativism</strong>: as a reaction, it holds that each culture can only be understood and evaluated by its own criteria, and that none is better than another. It has a positive side (it encourages respect), but taken to the extreme it is problematic: if everything is equally valid, we could not criticise unjust practices within any culture. Two senses should be distinguished. The <strong>methodological relativism</strong> of anthropology suspends judgement in order to understand a culture; <strong>moral relativism</strong> holds that there are no universal criteria. The first does not imply the second: one can understand a culture from within and defend, at the same time, certain universal minimums.</li> <li><strong>Interculturality</strong>: this is the most balanced attitude. It defends <strong>dialogue</strong> and mutual enrichment between cultures, on the basis of certain common minimums —such as <strong>human rights</strong> and the <strong>dignity</strong> of every person— that make it possible to respect difference without giving up criticism.</li> </ul>\n<p>A fourth attitude is usually added to these: <strong>universalism</strong>. There are values that hold for all cultures, such as the dignity of the person, and with them any practice can be judged. Its main argument is human rights; its main objection, that those values might be those of one particular culture presented as everyone’s. In 1947, while the Universal Declaration was being drafted, the American Anthropological Association warned of this danger. The debate is taken up again in <a href=\"#teoria/fil-t6\">Topic 6</a>, in the section on human rights.</p>\n<p><strong>Aporophobia.</strong> The philosopher <strong>Adela Cortina</strong> proposed in the 1990s a word for a form of rejection that had no name: <strong>aporophobia</strong>, from <em>áporos</em> (‘without resources’) and <em>phóbos</em> (‘fear, aversion’). She developed it in <em>Aporophobia, the Rejection of the Poor</em> (2017), and that same year the Royal Spanish Academy added it to its dictionary. Her observation is simple and uncomfortable: often it is not the foreigner who bothers people, but the poor foreigner. The tourist or the foreign footballer is welcomed with open arms; the immigrant without resources, with suspicion. For that reason Cortina asks us not to confuse aporophobia with xenophobia: the contempt is aimed at those who seem unable to give anything back, and it can also reach the poor of one’s own country.</p>\n<p>Living in a classroom, a neighbourhood or a city where different origins and customs coexist is a good opportunity to practise that intercultural outlook: neither shutting ourselves up in what is our own nor thinking that “it’s all the same”, but engaging in dialogue and looking for what unites us.</p>\n<p>The theory of evolution describes how species change, but it leaves open a philosophical question: does evolution have a direction? Here <strong>Teleology</strong> enters (from the Greek <em>télos</em>, ‘end’): explaining something by the goal towards which it tends. To say that seeds germinate ‘in order to’ produce a plant is a teleological explanation.</p><p>The French Jesuit and palaeontologist Pierre Teilhard de Chardin defended an evolution with a direction in <em>The Human Phenomenon</em>, written around 1940 and published in 1955, after his death. For him, the universe advances towards ever greater complexity and consciousness, in stages: the <strong>geosphere</strong>, inert matter; the <strong>biosphere</strong>, the layer of life; and the <strong>noosphere</strong> (from <em>noûs</em>, ‘mind’), the layer of thought that envelops the Earth with the appearance of the human being. Everything would converge towards a final point, which he called the <strong>Omega point</strong>.</p><p>The French biologist Jacques Monod, winner of the Nobel Prize in Medicine in 1965, replied in <em>Chance and Necessity</em> (1970) that there is no such finality. Mutations arise by <strong>chance</strong>, and natural selection, like a blind filter, preserves those that prove advantageous: that is <strong>necessity</strong>. Living beings seem made with a purpose, but that appearance is explained without any intention whatsoever.</p><p>Remember the case of the antibiotic-resistant bacteria you saw earlier: for Monod, chance mutations and an environment that favours some over others are enough; no one aims towards any goal. Whoever thinks like Teilhard will look at the whole, from atoms to cells and from cells to the human brain, and will see in that growing complexity a path towards the mind.</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: is it compatible to accept natural selection and at the same time believe that evolution has meaning? Is that a question that biology can answer?</p>\n<h3>From attitudes to policies: five models of living together</h3>\n<p>The attitudes above are personal: each of us has them towards whoever is different. But when communities with different languages, religions and customs live in the same country, the State also has to decide: which language is used in teaching, which holidays are working days, whether a religious symbol may be worn at work, or who decides the school canteen menu. The answers given over the twentieth and twenty-first centuries can be grouped into five models, according to what each one asks of minorities and of the majority.</p>\n<table> <thead> <tr> <th>Model</th> <th>What is asked of minorities</th> <th>An example</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td><strong>Segregation</strong></td> <td>Living apart: separate neighbourhoods, schools and rights for each group</td> <td>South African <em>apartheid</em> (1948-1994)</td> </tr> <tr> <td><strong>Assimilation</strong></td> <td>Leaving the culture of origin behind and adopting that of the majority in order to be equal citizens</td> <td>The French republican model, which does not recognise communities within the nation</td> </tr> <tr> <td><strong>Integration</strong></td> <td>Accepting some common public rules (laws, official language, rights) while keeping one’s own culture in private life</td> <td>The European Union’s Common Basic Principles on integration (2004), which define it as a two-way process</td> </tr> <tr> <td><strong>Multiculturalism</strong></td> <td>Nothing to give up: the State recognises groups and grants them some rights of their own</td> <td>Canada, which adopted it as official policy in 1971</td> </tr> <tr> <td><strong>Intercultural pluralism</strong></td> <td>Taking part in a dialogue in which all cultures, the majority one included, change by living together</td> <td>Quebec’s interculturalism (Bouchard-Taylor report, 2008)</td> </tr> </tbody> </table>\n<p>The most debated model is <strong>multiculturalism</strong>. Its defenders argue that equality is not achieved by treating everyone as if they were the same: the Canadian Will Kymlicka (<em>Multicultural Citizenship</em>, 1995) distinguishes between <strong>national minorities</strong>, peoples who already lived in a territory before becoming part of a larger State, and <strong>immigrant groups</strong>, and defends different rights for each case, such as self-government or schooling in their own language. Charles Taylor adds that denying someone recognition of their culture is a form of harm. Its critics reply from different angles. The liberal Brian Barry (<em>Culture and Equality</em>, 2001) believes that group rights divide citizens and that the law should be the same for everyone. In 1999 Susan Moller Okin asked whether multiculturalism is bad for women, because recognising a group can protect customs that oppress those who live within it. And in 2010 and 2011 several European leaders declared that this policy had failed, while others replied that it had never really been applied. The debate remains open.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: at your school there are students from many backgrounds. What should be the same for everyone and what could vary from family to family: the holiday calendar, the canteen menu, clothing, the language of lessons? Which model in the table does each answer correspond to? Think about Basque too: according to Kymlicka’s distinction, would its speakers be a national minority or just another group?</p>\n<p>Against the Enlightenment, which spoke of a culture and a reason universal to all humanity, the German <strong><a href=\"#biografias/herder\">Johann Gottfried Herder</a></strong> (1744-1803) stressed the opposite: each <strong>people</strong> (<em>Volk</em>) has its own spirit, the <strong><em>Volksgeist</em></strong>, expressed above all in its <strong>language</strong>, its songs and its customs. There is no single «correct» way to be human, but many cultures, each valuable in its own way. This idea nourished respect for diversity, but also, misunderstood, the nationalisms that turn «ours» into something superior.</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: make a list of ten agents that have socialised you (people, places, apps, music) and rank them from most to least influential; explain the first. Have you ever had a parasocial relationship? Could someone fall in love with a computer program, and would that be love?</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: are we defined above all by the culture and language we are born into, or is there something human common above them? Where is the border between loving one’s own culture and believing it better than the others? You will meet Herder in Year 2.</p>\n<h2>6. Consciousness, language and person</h2>\n<p>Beyond theories, there are some features that almost everyone recognises as deeply human. The first is <strong>consciousness</strong>: we do not merely perceive the world, we know that we perceive it. And we take one step further with <strong>self-consciousness</strong>, the capacity to turn back on ourselves and examine our own thoughts and feelings, which is called <strong>introspection</strong> (“looking inwards”). It was precisely this inner gaze that led Descartes to his famous certainty of “I think, therefore I am”.</p>\n<p>How can we tell whether a being has self-awareness? In 1970 the psychologist Gordon Gallup devised the <strong>mirror test</strong>: a mark is painted on the forehead of a sleeping animal, and researchers watch whether, on waking and seeing itself in a mirror, it touches the mark on its own body. Chimpanzees pass it, and so, according to some studies, do elephants, dolphins and magpies; children do at around eighteen months. The test has limits (an animal guided more by smell than by sight may fail it), but it shows that self-awareness is not all or nothing.</p>\n<p>The second feature is <strong>language</strong>. Many animals communicate, but only the human being possesses an <strong>articulate and symbolic language</strong>: from a limited number of meaningless sounds (phonemes) we are able to form infinite meaningful words and sentences. Linguistic signs are <strong>conventional</strong> and <strong>arbitrary</strong> (there is no natural relationship between the word “tree” and the object it names: it is an agreement shared by a whole community). Thanks to this <strong>symbolic capacity</strong> we can speak of what is not present, imagine the future, transmit culture and think abstractly. That is why language is not only an instrument: it is one of the conditions of our way of being.</p>\n<p>What is so special about human language compared with animal communication systems? The French linguist André Martinet (<em>Elementos de lingüística general</em>, 1960) called it <strong>double articulation</strong>. In a first articulation, utterances are divided into small units with meaning (in ‘gat-o-s’ there are three: the animal, the masculine and the plural). In a second articulation, those units are broken down into sounds without meaning, the <strong>phonemes</strong>: /g/, /a/, /t/, /o/, /s/. Changing just one gives a different word (‘gatos’, ‘patos’, ‘platos’), and with a few dozen phonemes a language builds its entire vocabulary. Animal systems do not work this way. Bees indicate with their dance the direction and distance of a food source, as Karl von Frisch showed (Nobel Prize in 1973), and vervet monkeys have distinct alarm calls for the leopard, the eagle and the snake; but each signal is a block that is neither broken down nor recombined to say something new. The linguist Charles Hockett (1960) added other features: <strong>productivity</strong> (we can say sentences nobody has ever said) and <strong>displacement</strong> (we talk about what is not here: yesterday, Mars or a dragon). The bees’ dance has something of displacement, because it speaks of a flower that is not in sight, but only of that: a bee cannot describe what last year’s flower looked like or invent one that does not exist.</p>\n<p>Where does that capacity come from? In 1957, Noam Chomsky (<em>Syntactic Structures</em>) and, two years later, in his scathing review of B. F. Skinner’s book <em>Verbal Behavior</em>, argued that language cannot be learnt through imitation and reinforcement alone, as the behaviourists thought. His most famous argument is that of the <strong>poverty of the stimulus</strong>: children hear incomplete, interrupted sentences in limited number, and yet, by the age of four or five, they master rules that no one has taught them. Their errors give them away: a child who says ‘I goed’ or ‘I breaked’ has not heard it from any adult; they are applying a general rule to an irregular verb. Chomsky concluded that we are born with a <strong>faculty of language</strong>, a kind of universal grammar that each concrete language merely ‘adjusts’. The nuances matter. Attempts to teach sign language to chimpanzees, such as Washoe (from 1966) or Nim Chimpsky, managed to get them to learn dozens of signs, but Herbert Terrace concluded in 1979 that Nim did not combine the signs with any syntax of his own. On the other hand, psychologists such as Michael Tomasello argue that innate grammar is unnecessary: general learning abilities would suffice, and above all the capacity to share attention and the intentions of others. The debate remains open, but both sides agree on one thing: no other species learns to speak the way any human child does.</p>\n<p><strong>Where did language come from?</strong> There are three classic ways of answering. According to the theory of <strong>agreement</strong>, or convention, words mean what they mean because speakers agreed on it; this is the thesis defended by Hermogenes in Plato’s <em>Cratylus</em>, against those who believe that names reflect the nature of things. According to the <strong>social</strong> theory, language arises and is learned in life with others: without other people to talk to, the capacity does not develop, as children raised in isolation show. According to the <strong>evolutionary</strong> theory, language appeared gradually, over hundreds of thousands of years, together with the brain and the vocal apparatus. The three are not entirely mutually exclusive: the capacity would be the fruit of evolution, and each particular language, a social convention.</p>\n<p><strong>Atapuerca and the ear.</strong> In the Sierra de Atapuerca (Burgos) there is a cavity reached by descending a shaft of about thirteen metres, the <strong>Sima de los Huesos</strong> (‘Pit of Bones’), where the remains of at least twenty-eight individuals who lived some 430,000 years ago, related to the Neanderthals, have been recovered. In 1992 <strong>Skull 5</strong> was found there, one of the most complete in the fossil record, known as ‘Miguelón’. In 2004, a team led by Ignacio Martínez reconstructed the ear ossicles of these hominins and concluded that their hearing was tuned to the frequencies in which human speech is concentrated, unlike that of chimpanzees. This does not prove that they spoke as we do, but it does show that they had the hearing of someone who listens to voices.</p>\n<p><strong>Which comes first, thinking or speaking?</strong> The relationship between language and thought has given rise to three positions. For some, language shapes thought: the linguist <a href=\"#biografias/sapir\">Edward Sapir</a> and his disciple <a href=\"#biografias/whorf\">Benjamin Lee Whorf</a> maintained that each language organises the world in its own way, and the sociologist Basil Bernstein linked the way each social class speaks to its way of reasoning. For others, thought comes first: Piaget saw language as an expression of an already formed intelligence, and Chomsky, as you have just read, as a faculty specific to the human brain. For the Russian psychologist Lev Vygotsky (<em>Thought and Language</em>, 1934), the two influence each other: they arise separately and around the age of two they intersect; from then on the child thinks by speaking, first aloud and later inwardly. Today a moderate version of the first thesis is accepted: language does not imprison thought, but it directs attention. Russian speakers, who have two basic words for light and dark blue, distinguish these shades slightly faster.</p>\n<table> <thead> <tr> <th>Position</th> <th>Authors</th> <th>Idea</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td><strong>Language shapes thought</strong></td> <td>Sapir, Whorf; Bernstein</td> <td>Each language, or each way of speaking, makes us see the world in a particular way</td> </tr> <tr> <td><strong>Thought comes first</strong></td> <td>Piaget, Chomsky</td> <td>Intelligence or the faculty develops first; language expresses it</td> </tr> <tr> <td><strong>They influence each other</strong></td> <td>Vygotsky</td> <td>Thought and language join around the age of two and from then on grow together</td> </tr> </tbody> </table>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t2-50\">From consciousness and reason there arises, lastly, the idea of <strong>person</strong>. The word comes from the Latin <em>persona</em>, which designated the actor’s mask in the theatre, but in philosophy it has acquired a much deeper sense: to be a person means to be a <strong>free</strong>, <strong>rational</strong> and <strong>responsible</strong> subject, and therefore the bearer of a <strong>dignity</strong> that has no price. The notion has Roman roots (Roman law already distinguished between persons and things) and Christian ones: in the 6th century, <strong>Boethius</strong> defined the person as ‘an individual substance of a rational nature’ (<em>Contra Eutychen</em>). Centuries later, Kant formulated this idea more forcefully than anyone, by asserting that we must never treat persons as mere things or instruments:</p>\n<p>Boethius’s definition deserves to be read slowly, word by word. Boethius wrote it around the year 512, in the treatise <em>Contra Eutychen et Nestorium</em> (chapter III), for a theological dispute: how Christ could have two natures, divine and human, and yet be a single person. But his formula, ‘individual substance of a rational nature’, served for everything else. <strong>Substance</strong>: the person is not a role being played nor a quality one has or loses, but someone who exists in their own right. <strong>Individual</strong>: not ‘humanity’ in general, but this one, unique and non-transferable. <strong>Of a rational nature</strong>: what decides it is the kind of being one is, not what one happens to be doing at this moment. Eleven centuries later, John Locke proposed another definition: a person is ‘a thinking intelligent being, that has reason and reflection, and can consider itself as itself, the same thinking thing, in different times and places’ (<em>An Essay Concerning Human Understanding</em>, II, 27, 9). The difference has consequences. For Boethius, a newborn or someone in a deep coma is a person, because their nature is rational even if they are not currently reasoning. For Locke, what is decisive is the consciousness one has of oneself, and whoever lacks it is, at the very least, a doubtful case. Much of the debate about borderline cases pits these two ways of understanding what a person is against each other.</p>\n<p><strong>Sensing and perceiving.</strong> Psychology distinguishes two levels. <strong>Sensation</strong> is the registering of a stimulus: something (a light, a sound) excites a receptor, the nerve carries the signal and the brain records it. Around 1835, the German physiologist Johannes Müller observed that each nerve always responds with its own quality: a blow to the eye is seen as a flash, not felt as a blow. Sensation is not, therefore, a copy of the world. Moreover, each sense has <strong>thresholds</strong>: below a certain intensity we notice nothing, and a dog hears frequencies that for us are silence. <strong>Perception</strong> organises these sensations into objects: we do not see patches of colour, but ‘a table’. The <strong>Gestalt</strong> school (‘form’, in German) showed in the early twentieth century that the whole is perceived before the parts, and that the mind always separates a <strong>figure</strong> from a <strong>ground</strong>. In the ambiguous image popularised by the Dane Edgar Rubin in 1915 one can see a vase or two faces in profile facing each other, but never both at once.</p>\n<p>For centuries knowledge was imagined as a chain in two steps: first the senses collect data and then reason orders them. The Basque philosopher Xavier Zubiri discussed this in his trilogy <em>Sentient Intelligence</em> (1980-1983). For him there are not two separate faculties: human intelligence is <strong>sentient</strong> and our sensing is already <strong>intelligent</strong>. An animal grasps things as stimuli that provoke a response; the human being grasps them as <strong>real</strong>, as something that is “of itself”, that exists by itself and not only in relation to what I need.</p><p>If you smell smoke at home, you do not first register a neutral odour and then deduce that something is burning: in the very act of smelling you already grasp something real out there, a possible fire, and that is why you jump off the sofa.</p><p>The Frenchman Edgar Morin, in <em>Introduction to Complex Thinking</em> (1990) and in his multi-volume work <em>The Method</em>, extends this view to the whole human being. We are <strong><em>sapiens</em></strong>, capable of reasoning and calculating, but also <strong><em>demens</em></strong>: we carry within us passion, imagination, taste for risk, fear and even madness. Reducing ourselves to pure reason impoverishes us, because from that mixture come both delusions and art or science. That is why Morin calls for a <strong>complex thinking</strong>, which does not separate what in reality goes together.</p><p>Think of someone who decides to set up a small business: they do the sums and study the market, but they are also moved by the excitement of creating something of their own, and held back by the fear of losing their savings. Without the calculation it would be madness; without the excitement, they would not even try.</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: can something important be decided only with reason, without emotion? Do you remember a decision of yours in which what you felt weighed more than what you thought?</p>\n<p><strong>How intelligence grows.</strong> The Swiss psychologist <strong>Jean Piaget</strong> (1896-1980) observed children for decades, beginning with his own, and concluded that intelligence is not filled up like a container, but is built in stages: the child does not know less than the adult, but thinks in a different way. He summarised this in <em>The Psychology of Intelligence</em> (1947). Later research has shown that many abilities appear earlier than he believed and that the stages are not as clear-cut, but his central idea still stands: thinking is an activity that develops.</p>\n<table> <thead> <tr> <th>Approximate age</th> <th>Stage</th> <th>What is achieved</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>0-2 years</td> <td><strong>Sensorimotor</strong></td> <td>Knowing by acting: touching, sucking, moving; by the end, knowing that an object still exists even when it cannot be seen</td> </tr> <tr> <td>2-7 years</td> <td><strong>Preoperational</strong></td> <td>Language, symbolic play, drawing; thought still fixes on a single aspect of things</td> </tr> <tr> <td>7-11 years</td> <td><strong>Concrete operations</strong></td> <td>Classifying, ordering, understanding that a quantity is conserved even if its shape changes; reasoning with what is in front of one</td> </tr> <tr> <td>From 11-12 years</td> <td><strong>Formal operations</strong></td> <td>Thinking abstractly, formulating hypotheses (‘what if…?’) and reasoning about one’s own ideas</td> </tr> </tbody> </table>\n<p><strong>Intelligence and freedom.</strong> What, then, distinguishes human intelligence from animal intelligence? The philosopher José Antonio Marina (<em>Theory of Creative Intelligence</em>, 1993) proposes a formula: human intelligence is animal intelligence transformed by freedom. A chimpanzee uses a stick to extract termites, and that is intelligence; but it does not set out to invent new uses for the stick, nor does it set itself goals that nobody has given it. We do: we choose what to think and for what purpose. To this is added a feature you already know: human beings not only know, but <strong>know that they know</strong>, and can think about their own thinking in order to correct it.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\">\n<p>“Act in such a way that you treat humanity, whether in your own person or in the person of any other, always at the same time as an end, never merely as a means.” — Immanuel Kant, <em>Groundwork of the Metaphysics of Morals</em> (1785)</p> </blockquote>\n<p>Recognising that every person is an end in themselves —and not an object that can be used or discarded— is the basis of human rights and of a just coexistence.</p>\n<p><strong>Equality is built.</strong> The philosopher <a href=\"#biografias/arendt\">Hannah Arendt</a>, who fled Nazi Germany and lived for years as a stateless person, added an important nuance. In <em>The Origins of Totalitarianism</em> (1951) she maintains that we are not born equal: we are born different, with different talents, bodies and fortunes. We become equal because, as members of a political community, we decide to guarantee one another the same rights. That is why the stateless person, who belongs to no community, is left without rights even though still a human being: such a person lacks what Arendt called the right to have rights. Dignity may belong to everyone; equality must be upheld by everyone.</p>\n<p>Kant explained the difference between a person and a thing with an economic distinction. In the <em>Groundwork of the Metaphysics of Morals</em> (second section) he writes, more or less: in the kingdom of ends, everything has either a <strong>price</strong> or a <strong>dignity</strong>; what has a price can be replaced by something equivalent, whereas what is above all price, and admits no equivalent, has dignity. Your phone has a price: if it breaks, an identical one can replace it. Your best friend does not: no one would say that some other ‘equivalent’ person could take her place. It is worth reading the formula of humanity carefully. Kant does not forbid using others as means, which we do every day: the bus driver serves me as a means of getting to school. He forbids treating them <strong>merely</strong> as means, that is, in a way to which they could not give their consent. His example is the false promise: someone who borrows money promising to repay it, knowing they will not, turns the other person into a mere tool, because that person would never accept the deal if they knew the truth. The same test is used today in highly debated cases, such as the sale of organs or certain employment contracts: is there genuine consent or only necessity? Philosophers are divided over the answers, but the question is Kant’s.</p>\n<p>If being a person gives dignity, it matters a great deal to know <strong>who is one</strong>, and this is where the borderline cases appear. Is an embryo a few weeks old a person? Is someone in an irreversible coma still one? Could a chimpanzee, which recognises itself in a mirror and learns signs, be one, or a robot that holds conversations? In 2017 Saudi Arabia granted citizenship to a robot called Sophia, and many saw it as a propaganda gesture rather than a serious recognition. The philosopher Mary Anne Warren (1973) proposed a list of traits: consciousness, the capacity to reason, self-motivated activity, communication and self-awareness. The problem is that these traits are <strong>gradual</strong>: they appear little by little during development and are lost little by little through illness. That is why other authors argue that belonging to the human species is enough. The answers to these questions divide society in debates such as those on abortion, euthanasia or animal rights, and in them there are serious reasons on more than one side.</p>\n<p>That the word ‘person’ does not quite coincide with ‘human being’ is best seen in law. The Spanish Civil Code calls associations and companies <strong>legal persons</strong> (article 35): a company can sign contracts, have debts and be convicted, though it does not feel or think. And since Law 17/2021, the same code states that animals are ‘living beings endowed with sensitivity’ (article 333 bis): they are no longer mere things, but neither are they persons. Philosophers debate whether that intermediate category is enough. Tom Regan (<em>The Case for Animal Rights</em>, 1983) argues that every ‘subject of a life’, with desires, memory and a future that matters to it, has inherent value and rights, such as the right not to be treated as a resource. In 1993, the Great Ape Project, led by Paola Cavalieri and Peter Singer, called for basic rights such as life and liberty for chimpanzees, gorillas and orangutans. On the other side, the Kantian tradition reserves rights for those who can have duties, that is, for those capable of answering for their actions; Kant did not approve of cruelty to animals, but condemned it because whoever is cruel to them becomes hardened towards people as well (<em>Lectures on Ethics</em>). Both positions carry a cost: if rights depend on capacities, some human beings would fall outside them; if they depend on species, one must explain why species matters morally.</p>\n<p>In the 20th century, <strong><a href=\"#biografias/husserl\">Edmund Husserl</a></strong> (1859-1938) made consciousness the centre of philosophy and founded <strong>phenomenology</strong>. His starting point is that all consciousness is always <strong>consciousness <em>of</em> something</strong>: when I think, I think of something; when I fear, I fear something. He called this directedness of the mind towards its objects <strong>intentionality</strong>. Against theories that explained the mind by hidden causes, he proposed a humbler method: carefully <strong>describing</strong> how things appear to us, placing “in brackets” what we take for granted about them. His motto sums up the programme: to return “to the things themselves”, to experience as it is lived before any theory.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\">\n<p>To the things themselves! — Edmund Husserl, <em>Logical Investigations</em> (1900-1901)</p> </blockquote>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: do you know words in Basque, Spanish or another language that cannot be translated well into others? Do they change the way you see things? Try to solve a problem without ‘talking to yourself’ inwardly: can you think without words?</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: when you see a table, do you see “the table” as a whole or only one face, from where you stand? How much of what you “perceive” is actually supplied by your mind? Much of the philosophy of Year 2 stems from Husserl, such as the existentialism of Sartre and Heidegger.</p>\n<h2>7. The problem of personal identity</h2>\n<p>There is a question that seems simple and is not at all: what makes me remain <strong>the same person</strong> over time? My body changes ceaselessly (cells are renewed, we grow, we age), my ideas and tastes today are not those of my childhood, and yet we say that I am “the same”. What is that <strong>identity</strong> based on?</p>\n<p>An ancient puzzle illustrates the problem: the <strong>ship of Theseus</strong>. If we replace a ship’s planks one by one until all of them are replaced, is it still the same ship? The same can be asked about ourselves. Two senses of ‘the same’ should be distinguished: <strong>numerical identity</strong> (being the same individual, one and not two) and <strong>qualitative identity</strong> (having the same characteristics). Two twins can be almost alike and be two people; I, who have changed so much, remain numerically the same. Philosophers have proposed different criteria:</p>\n<ul> <li><strong>Memory</strong>: I am the same because I remember my past and recognise myself in it. This is the criterion stressed by <strong>Locke</strong>: what gives unity to the self is the continuity of consciousness and of memories.</li> <li><strong>Bodily continuity</strong>: I am the same because, despite the changes, my body maintains a continuity in space and time.</li> <li>The <strong>narrative</strong> I tell about myself: I construct my identity as a story that links what I was, what I am and what I want to become.</li> </ul>\n<p>The memory criterion met with objections. <strong>Thomas Reid</strong> pointed out that memory does not <em>create</em> identity, it only <em>shows</em> it, and that it leads to absurdities: a general remembers having been a young officer, and the officer remembered having been a boy who was punished, but the general does not remember the boy; according to Locke, he would and would not be the same person. Moreover, the baby you were was you, even though you do not remember it. Today a broader <strong>psychological continuity</strong> is preferred, but the case of <strong>fission</strong> (two people who inherit your mental life) led <strong>Derek Parfit</strong> to think that what matters to us in surviving may not be identity.</p>\n<p>What weighs more, the body or the mind? Locke proposed a thought experiment: if the soul of a prince, with all his memories, entered the body of a cobbler, we would all see that it is the prince who is still there, even though outwardly he looks like the cobbler (<em>Essay</em>, II, 27, 15). This is the <strong>psychological criterion</strong>: I am where my memories, my character and my projects are. Many films about swapped bodies take that intuition for granted. Bernard Williams (<em>The Self and the Future</em>, 1970) showed that the intuition changes according to how the case is told. Imagine being told: ‘Tomorrow you will be tortured; first we will erase your memories and give you those of another person.’ It would be no comfort: you would still fear the pain, because it would be your body, and therefore you, who would suffer it. Yet the situation is the same as the swap, merely told from the inside. This is the <strong>bodily criterion</strong>: I am this organism, which remains alive even though what it remembers changes. The psychological criterion explains well why memory and character matter to us; the bodily one explains why we fear what happens to our body even when we are promised a change of mind. That one and the same person feels both intuitions depending on the story shows that the problem is not resolved.</p>\n<p>Derek Parfit (<em>Reasons and Persons</em>, 1984, part three) took the problem into science fiction with the <strong>teletransporter</strong>. You step into a booth on Earth; a scanner records the exact state of all your cells and, in doing so, destroys them; the information travels to Mars at the speed of light, and there a machine builds, from new matter, a body identical to yours, with your brain, your memories and your plans. The person who emerges on Mars swears that it is you and that the journey was extremely quick. Have you travelled, or have you died and someone else has begun to live? Parfit complicates the case: imagine that one day the scanner malfunctions and does not destroy your body on Earth. There are then two identical people, and the one on Mars cannot be you, because you are still here. But then, why would it be you when the scanner works properly? Parfit’s conclusion is surprising: what truly matters to us is not identity, but <strong>psychological continuity and connectedness</strong> with whoever we will be, and these hold equally on Mars. For him, being destroyed and replicated would be almost as good as surviving in the usual way. Many readers resist: they would step into the booth to send a copy, not to travel themselves.</p>\n<p>The classical definition of the human being is that of <strong>Rational animal</strong>. The German philosopher Ernst Cassirer found it too narrow, and in <em>Philosophical anthropology</em> (in the original, <em>An Essay on Man</em>, 1944) he proposed another: we are a <strong>symbolic animal</strong>. Reason does not explain everything, because a good part of our life does not consist in reasoning, but in giving meaning to things.</p><p>According to Cassirer, the human being does not live only in a physical world, like other animals. Between him and reality he has woven a net, a <strong>symbolic universe</strong>: culture. Its main threads are <strong>Myth</strong> and religion, <strong>Language</strong> (of which <strong>Science</strong> is a more precise development), <strong>art</strong> and history. We no longer touch reality “raw”: we always look at it through those forms.</p><p>A flag is a piece of coloured cloth, and a coin, a piece of metal; but no one treats them as such, because they are worth what they symbolise. The same happens with the song that a group of friends sings at the end of every party: for someone who does not know it, it is just music; for them it means everything they have lived together.</p><p>That is why Cassirer distinguishes the <strong>sign</strong> or signal from the <strong>symbol</strong>. A dog learns that the noise of the lead announces a walk: it is a signal linked to a concrete situation, which provokes a response. The symbol, on the other hand, has a conventional and open meaning: I can use the word “walk” to remember one from years ago, imagine an impossible one or write a poem. You have already seen that human language is symbolic; Cassirer turns that capacity into the key to everything human.</p><p class=\"aporia\">Something to think about: could we live without symbols, only with what we perceive directly? What would remain of your day if you removed everything that is worth what it means?</p>\n<p>Locke also made clear what the word is for: ‘person’ is a <strong>forensic</strong> term (<em>An Essay Concerning Human Understanding</em>, II, 27), that is, it serves to attribute actions, merits and blame; I can only answer for what I did. Against him, the <strong>animalism</strong> of Eric Olson (<em>The Human Animal</em>, 1997) holds that each of us is, quite simply, a human organism: the foetus you came from was you, even though it had no memories or consciousness. There is also a problem that looks not at time but at the present: is there always <strong>one person per body</strong>? In the 1960s, Roger Sperry and Michael Gazzaniga studied patients whose connection between the two hemispheres had been cut to treat their epilepsy, and in some tests each half seemed to know things the other did not. And the ship of Theseus has a second part, raised by Hobbes: if someone kept the old planks and built another ship with them, which of the two would be Theseus’s?</p>\n<p>Not everyone has thought that a permanent ‘self’ exists. <strong>Hume</strong>, for example, rejected the existence of a soul or stable substance: in his view, the self is nothing more than a collection of successive perceptions and states. Hume does not dispute that we feel continuity; what he denies is that this continuity rests on a substance. Moreover, identity is not only <strong>individual</strong> but also <strong>social</strong>: we define ourselves in relation to others, to the groups we belong to and to the culture we have inherited.</p>\n<p><strong>Kinds of identity and how it is built.</strong> The question “who am I?” has two sides: what makes me the same person over time (<strong>continuity</strong>) and what sets me apart from others (<strong>singularity</strong>). To put it in order, four kinds of identity can be distinguished:</p><ul><li><strong>Biological:</strong> DNA, facial features, the presence of a body.</li><li><strong>Psychological:</strong> memory, character, the way of perceiving.</li><li><strong>Social:</strong> name, gender, nationality, language, social class.</li><li><strong>Narrative:</strong> for Paul Ricœur (<em>Oneself as Another</em>, 1990), each person understands themselves by telling their life as a story that links memories and plans; we are, in part, the account we give of ourselves.</li></ul><p>Identity is not given once and for all: it is constantly built and negotiated, between what family, school and society give us and what each of us chooses. The sociologist Zygmunt Bauman (<em>Liquid Modernity</em>, 2000) added that in today's societies, with so much mobility and so many options, identity has become <strong>liquid</strong>: less fixed, more provisional, something that has to be remade again and again.</p>\n<p><strong>Identity crises.</strong> The expression was popularised by the psychoanalyst Erik Erikson (<em>Identity: Youth and Crisis</em>, 1968). For him, each stage of life poses a task, and that of adolescence is to build an identity of one’s own in the face of role confusion. It is a stage of tensions: you want to belong to the group and, at the same time, to be unique; you re-examine what you learned at home; sometimes the image you project does not match the one you have of yourself, and this produces anxiety or a feeling of emptiness. But crises are not exclusive to youth: a house move, a loss or a change of job can shake identity at any age. Erikson did not see them as a failure, but as moments of decision from which one emerges with a firmer identity.</p>\n<p><strong>Identity on social media.</strong> On social media we show a chosen version of ourselves: the best photos, the filters, the good days. This <strong>virtual identity</strong> is not entirely false, but nor is it complete, and it has effects on the real one: digital reputation endures, and comparing oneself daily with retouched versions of others weighs on one’s own image. The South Korean-German philosopher <a href=\"#biografias/han\">Byung-Chul Han</a> goes further. In <em>Psychopolitics</em> (2014) he compares the mobile phone to an object of devotion, which we look at many times a day, and the ‘like’ to a digital amen: a way of assenting without thinking. According to Han, it is no longer necessary for anyone to watch over us or force us: we expose and exploit ourselves voluntarily, convinced that this is how we are free. It can be objected that he exaggerates and that social media also serve to find others, organise or learn. But the question remains: who decides how I present myself, I or the algorithm that rewards some images and hides others?</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: what does your social media profile have in common with how you are in class or at home, and what separates them? Do you find it easy to use your phone in moderation? Suggest three concrete ideas for achieving this and choose one to try this week.</p>\n<p>To go further: the dilemma <a href=\"#dilemas/fil-fision\">‘The two who wake up’</a> puts the criteria of this section to the test with an imaginary case: your two cerebral hemispheres, transplanted into two bodies, wake up with your very memories. Derek Parfit (<em>Reasons and Persons</em>, 1984) used cases like this to argue that what matters to us in surviving is not so much identity as psychological continuity. And if the criterion is memory, it is worth knowing how fragile it is: in <a href=\"#teoria/fil-t3\">Topic 3</a> you will see that a memory is not a recording but a reconstruction that can be mistaken.</p>\n<p>Today the problem also has a new dimension. <strong>Transhumanism</strong> is a current that advocates using technology (genetics, artificial intelligence, prostheses or even fusion with machines) to overcome the limits of the human body and “improve” our species. This reopens the old question with unprecedented force: if we deeply transformed our body and mind, would we still be “us”? Where is the core of what we are? The Spanish thinker Ortega y Gasset summed up the connection between the self and its surroundings in a formula:</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\">\n<p>“I am myself and my circumstance.” — José Ortega y Gasset, <em>Meditations on Quixote</em> (1914)</p> </blockquote>\n<p>That is to say: we are not an isolated self, but a self that always lives in specific circumstances —an era, a body, a society— with which it makes itself.</p>\n<p><strong>Bioconservatives and transhumanists.</strong> The question of the limits of technical enhancement is no longer science fiction. Since 2004 there have been people with paralysis who move a cursor with brain implants; since 2012 the CRISPR technique has made it possible to edit specific genes with unprecedented precision, and in 2018 two girls with a modified genome were born in China, in an experiment that the scientific community condemned. Faced with these possibilities, two positions confront each other. The <strong>transhumanists</strong>, such as the Swedish philosopher <a href=\"#biografias/bostrom\">Nick Bostrom</a>, advocate using technology to overcome disease, ageing and perhaps death. The <strong>bioconservatives</strong>, such as Francis Fukuyama (<em>Our Posthuman Future</em>, 2002) or Michael Sandel (<em>The Case Against Perfection</em>, 2007), fear that, by redesigning ourselves, we may lose equality, freedom or the humility of accepting what has been given to us. Behind this lie two uncomfortable questions. If enhancements are expensive, will only a few have them, as in the film <em>Gattaca</em> (1997), where each person is assigned a job according to their genes? And if they are manufactured by large technology companies, will it be they who decide what a ‘better’ human being is?</p>\n<table> <thead> <tr> <th>Position</th> <th>What it proposes</th> <th>What it hopes for or fears</th> <th>Authors</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td><strong>Transhumanism</strong></td> <td>To overcome with technology the limits of the body: disease, old age, death</td> <td>Hopes for an ‘enhanced’ human being, healthier, longer-lived and more intelligent</td> <td>Nick Bostrom</td> </tr> <tr> <td><strong>Bioconservatism</strong></td> <td>To protect human nature as it is and to set limits on enhancement</td> <td>Fears the loss of equality, freedom and dignity</td> <td>Francis Fukuyama, Michael Sandel, Jürgen Habermas</td> </tr> </tbody> </table>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: would you have an implant fitted to improve your memory? And to stop feeling fear? Explain where you would draw the line and why. Imagine that in fifty years’ time only the rich can afford genetic enhancements: what would happen to equality, which is what Sustainable Development Goal 10 seeks? And remember Haraway: your glasses or your phone, are they already part of you?</p>\n<p>Something to think about: thought experiments have a real counterpart in amnesia. The British musician Clive Wearing suffered an encephalitis in 1985 that destroyed much of his memory: since then he retains nothing for more than a few seconds and barely remembers his earlier life. Each time he sees his wife he greets her with the joy of someone who has not seen her for years, yet he still knows she is his wife, he keeps his character, and he is able to play the piano and conduct a choir. Something similar happened to Henry Molaison, who had brain surgery in 1953: he could not form new memories, but the psychologist Brenda Milner found that he learned to draw by looking in a mirror and improved day by day, without remembering ever having practised. There are, therefore, different kinds of memory: that of lived episodes and that of skills and habits. Is Clive Wearing still the same person he was before 1985? What criterion do you use in answering: the body, the memory of episodes, character, love for his wife? And if you lost all your memories but not your way of being, which would seem more serious to you: losing your past or losing your manner of reacting to the world?</p>\n<h2>8. The meaning of existence</h2>\n<p>We thus arrive at the deepest and most personal question of all: does human life have <strong>meaning</strong>? It is a question that science cannot answer with a piece of data, because it is not a matter of describing the world, but of deciding how to live in it. Basically, three kinds of answer have been given.</p>\n<p><strong>What are we asking when we ask about meaning?</strong> The word <em>meaning</em> has at least three senses, and it is worth keeping them apart. It can mean <strong>purpose</strong>: something has meaning if it is for something, like an arrow pointing in a direction. It can mean <strong>value</strong>: what is worthwhile has meaning, even if it is not for anything else, like a friendship. And it can mean <strong>intelligibility</strong>: a sentence has meaning if it can be understood, and a life has it if its parts form a story rather than a heap of loose episodes. We must also distinguish two questions that are often confused. One is the question of the meaning <strong>of</strong> life: whether the universe, the human species or existence as a whole has a purpose. The other is the question of meaning <strong>in</strong> life: what makes a particular person's life worth living. One can answer ‘no’ to the first and ‘yes’ to the second, and that is what many atheist thinkers do; or one can think, like many believers, that the second depends on the first. Keep this distinction in mind as you read the three answers that follow.</p>\n<p data-bloque=\"fil-t2-67\">The first claims that life has <strong>no meaning</strong>, that it is <strong>absurd</strong>. This is the position of certain existentialists, such as <strong>Camus</strong>, who compared it with the myth of <strong>Sisyphus</strong> (condemned to push a rock up a mountain for eternity only to watch it fall again and again), or of the early <strong>Sartre</strong>, who described the human being as a being “thrown into the world” with no prior justification.</p>\n<p><strong>Camus: the absurd and revolt.</strong> <em>The Myth of Sisyphus</em> (1942) opens without leaving any way out: the only truly serious philosophical problem is suicide, because deciding whether life is worth it is answering the fundamental question of philosophy. For Albert Camus (1913-1960), the absurd lies neither in the human being nor in the world, but in their clash: we demand clarity, reasons, a meaning, and the world answers with a silence that is not reasonable. Faced with this clash, there are, in his view, two escapes. One is physical suicide, which does away with the question by doing away with the one who asks it. The other is what he calls <strong>philosophical suicide</strong>: taking a leap towards a hope that promises a meaning beyond, as, in his judgement, Kierkegaard or <a href=\"#biografias/jaspers\">Jaspers</a> do. Camus rejects both and proposes keeping one's eyes open before the absurd, without denying it. From this he draws three consequences: <strong>revolt</strong> (refusing to resign oneself), <strong>freedom</strong> (there is no written script to follow) and <strong>passion</strong> (living each moment intensely). That is why the book ends with a surprising image: Sisyphus, walking back down for his rock knowing that it will roll down again, can be happy, because his lucidity and his effort belong to him. ‘One must imagine Sisyphus happy’.</p>\n<p><strong>Nagel: the absurd without the drama.</strong> Thomas Nagel examined the question in a short and much-cited article, ‘The Absurd’ (1971). First he takes apart some common arguments. It is said, for example, that nothing we do will matter in a million years. But, Nagel replies, if that is true, then what will happen in a million years does not matter now either, including the fact that nothing of today will matter then. It is also said that our life is absurd because it is very short or because we are tiny in the universe; but if we lived for ever or were enormous, our lives would not have more meaning for that. For Nagel, the absurd comes from somewhere else: from the clash between the seriousness with which we take our lives (exams, appearance, friendships) and the capacity, which only human beings have, to step back and look at all of it from outside, as something arbitrary that could be otherwise. We can neither stop taking it seriously nor stop taking that step back. That is why he sees no reason either for despair or for Camus's heroic defiance, which he finds romantic and slightly self-pitying: he proposes living our absurd lives with <strong>irony</strong>.</p>\n<p>The second maintains that life <strong>does have meaning</strong>, and here two great routes are distinguished. That of <strong>transcendent meaning</strong>, characteristic of religions and of currents such as Christian personalism, which places ultimate meaning <em>beyond</em> this life (in God, in salvation). And that of <strong>immanent meaning</strong>, defended by evolutionist, ecologist and vitalist currents or by Marxist humanism, which finds meaning <em>in this very life</em>: in developing our capacities, in work, in our relationship with others and with nature.</p>\n<p>An example of the transcendent sense is the <strong>personalism</strong> of the Frenchman <strong>Emmanuel Mounier</strong> (1905-1950), founder of the journal <em>Esprit</em> (1932) and author of <em>A Personalist Manifesto</em> (1936). Against individualism, which isolates, and against the totalitarianisms of his time, which dissolved the person into the mass, Mounier maintained that each person has an absolute value, that it is realised in relation with others and that it is open to transcendence. As an example of the immanent sense, it is enough to think of Marxism: meaning lies in this life, in transforming an unjust society.</p>\n<p><strong>Tolstoy: ‘and then what?’.</strong> Leo Tolstoy (1828-1910) had everything when, at nearly fifty, he had already written <em>War and Peace</em> and <em>Anna Karenina</em>: fame, a family he loved, land and health. And yet he tells in his <em>Confession</em> (written around 1880) how a question began to paralyse him. Every time he thought about his plans, a voice asked him, more or less: ‘You will have more land than anyone and you will be more famous than Shakespeare. And then what?’. To describe his situation he recalls an old Eastern fable: a traveller, fleeing from a wild beast, hides in a dry well, at the bottom of which a dragon is waiting for him; he clings to a branch and sees that two mice, one white and one black, day and night, are gnawing through it; meanwhile, he licks a few drops of honey from the leaves. The honey, what used to sweeten his life (family, art), no longer comforted him. Tolstoy even hid a rope so as not to hang himself. His way out was faith, not the faith of the drawing rooms but that of the simple people of the countryside, who lived and died with serenity: a finite life, he concluded, only has meaning if it is joined to something infinite. It is a clear example of the answer of <strong>transcendent meaning</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Debate: does meaning need God?</strong> Those who say yes often reason like Tolstoy. If everything ends with death and, in the end, with the burning out of the universe, no achievement lasts, and what does not last at all seems to be worth nothing; only a God who preserves and judges what we do would give each life a value that is not lost. Besides, religions offer something few philosophies offer: a story that explains where we come from, where we are going and why suffering is not the last word. Those who say no reply with several reasons. The philosopher Kurt Baier, in a lecture entitled ‘The Meaning of Life’ (1957), objected that attributing to a human being a purpose in that sense, the sense in which a tool has one, is to degrade them: nobody wants to be the instrument of someone else's plan. Others point out that duration does not confer value: a concert is not worth less because it comes to an end. And many believers, for their part, live meaning both in God and in this life, in love or in work well done, without setting them against each other. There is no compulsory answer here: what you can do is examine which of these reasons convinces you, and why.</p>\n<p>The third answer invites <strong>each person</strong> to give meaning to their own life. If we are <strong>freedom</strong> and <strong>project</strong>, then meaning is not given in advance: it has to be built through our decisions and commitments. In the face of what some psychologists have called the <strong>existential vacuum</strong> —that feeling of meaninglessness and deep boredom— the way out is not to flee towards money or immediate pleasure, but to find goals and values worth living for. And that task, precisely because we are free, is also a <strong>responsibility</strong>: choosing who I want to be and what I want to do with my life. It is one option among the three: none is the obligatory conclusion of the topic, and each has its reasons.</p>\n<p><strong>Frankl: a why to live for.</strong> The Viennese psychiatrist Viktor Frankl (1905-1997) was deported in 1942 and passed through several Nazi concentration camps, Auschwitz among them; in the camps his parents, his brother and his wife died. In 1946 he published the book known in English as <em>Man's Search for Meaning</em>. In it he observes that, in similar conditions, those who held on to something to live for held out better: a person waiting for them outside, a work to finish. He summed it up with a sentence from Nietzsche (<em>Twilight of the Idols</em>, ‘Maxims and Arrows’, 12): whoever has a why to live for can bear almost any how. From this came <strong>logotherapy</strong>, a therapy centred on meaning, and from this also comes the expression <strong>existential vacuum</strong>, which he coined. According to Frankl, meaning is found along three paths: by doing something (a job, a work), by loving someone or experiencing something valuable, and, when suffering is unavoidable, through the attitude with which one faces it. Be careful with this last point: Frankl does not say that suffering is good. If a suffering can be avoided, the sensible thing is to avoid it; only when there is no way out does the freedom to decide how to bear it remain.</p>\n<p>Frankl observed that the <strong>existential vacuum</strong> is not found only in extreme situations. He also described it as a <strong>Sunday neurosis</strong>: when the week ends and the rush stops, some people are overcome by a feeling of boredom and of nothing mattering, which they try to cover up with money, shopping, immediate pleasure or noise. For him this does not work, because meaning is not manufactured at will: it is discovered. That is why he turned the question around. It is not a matter of what we can expect from life, but of what life expects from us: every situation poses a question, and we answer with what we do.</p>\n<p><strong>Wolf: when what draws you is worthwhile.</strong> The American philosopher Susan Wolf put forward in <em>Meaning in Life and Why It Matters</em> (2010) an answer that combines two of the previous ones. For her, a life has meaning when <strong>subjective attraction</strong> meets <strong>objective value</strong>: when someone gives themselves eagerly to something that is truly worthwhile. Both conditions are necessary, and this is easy to see by thinking about what happens when one is missing. Imagine someone who spends every afternoon, with genuine enthusiasm, counting the blades of grass in the park: they are engaged, but in something without value; their life does not seem to have more meaning because of it. Now imagine someone who works in a hospital saving lives, but who experiences it as someone else's burden, without caring about it at all: they do something valuable, but they are alienated from it. The first person has passion without value; the second, value without passion. Meaning appears when the two come together. The proposal has its critics: who decides what has objective value? Is it not elitist to say that the life of someone who enjoys something humble has less meaning? Wolf replies that value does not require greatness: looking after an allotment, a grandfather or the local football team can have plenty of it.</p>\n<p><strong>A happy life and a meaningful life.</strong> We tend to use the two expressions as if they were the same, but they are not. Think of two cases. One person lives comfortably, has fun, has no problems and is satisfied, but nothing they do really matters to anyone, not even to themselves. Another looks after her sick mother for years, sleeps little and is often exhausted and sad, but would not change what she does for anything. The first seems happier; the second, fuller of meaning. A study by the psychologist Roy Baumeister and his colleagues (2013) found something similar in surveys: happiness was associated above all with satisfying needs and wants and with the present, while meaning was associated with linking past and future, with giving to others, and also with more worry and stress. That is why Susan Wolf speaks of meaning as a third dimension of the good life, distinct from happiness and from morality. This does not mean that one has to choose: in Aristotle's <em>eudaimonía</em>, which you will see in Topic 5, happiness and a worthwhile life went together. But the distinction helps us understand why there are people who, having everything to be content, feel that something is missing.</p>\n<p>A century before the existentialists, <strong><a href=\"#biografias/schopenhauer\">Arthur Schopenhauer</a></strong> (1788-1860) had given the bleakest answer to the question of meaning. In <em>The World as Will and Representation</em> (1819) he holds that, beneath appearances, the ground of everything is not reason but a blind <strong>will</strong> to live: a goalless impulse that drives us to desire without rest. And there lies the problem: when we do not get what we want, we suffer; when we do, <strong>boredom</strong> sets in. That is why life swings, like a pendulum, between pain and boredom. As ways out he proposed art (which gives a respite, since we contemplate without desiring), compassion and the renunciation of one’s own willing. His pessimism deeply influenced <strong>Nietzsche</strong> and <strong>Freud</strong>, whom you will meet in Year 2.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\">\n<p>Life swings, like a pendulum, between pain and boredom. — Arthur Schopenhauer, <em>The World as Will and Representation</em> (1819), § 57</p> </blockquote>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To think about: do we desire because something is lacking, so that satisfaction lasts little and we soon want something else? Is there a way of willing that does not lead to that back-and-forth?</p>\n<p><strong>Death as a horizon.</strong> As far as we know, we are the only animal that knows it is going to die, and from this awareness spring, to a large extent, religions, philosophy and much of art. No reflection on meaning can dodge it. In <em>The Death of Ivan Ilyich</em> (1886), Tolstoy tells how a dying judge remembers the syllogism he had learnt at school: ‘Caius is a man, men are mortal, therefore Caius is mortal’. It had always seemed correct to him applied to Caius, but never to himself, to Ivan, the little boy who played and loved his mother. Martin Heidegger cites this novella in <em>Being and Time</em> (1927, § 51), where he describes the human being as <strong>being-towards-death</strong>. Usually, he says, we speak of death impersonally: ‘one dies’, as if it were something that happens to others and that, for us, is always put off until later. But death is each person's ownmost possibility: no one can die in my place, it is certain and, at the same time, I do not know when it will come. Taking it on without fleeing, instead of covering it up, makes it possible to live <strong>authentically</strong>: to understand one's own life as a limited whole and to take one's decisions seriously, instead of letting them be decided by what ‘one’ does. Epicurus thought that death is nothing to us, and Nagel replied to him; you will find that debate in Topic 5.</p>\n<p><strong>Something to think about: three lives.</strong> Read these three descriptions and apply what you have seen. A girl your age spends almost all her free time playing online video games; she is very good, she enjoys herself and she says she is happy. A boy gives up football, which he loved, to help every afternoon in the family business, which bores him, because at home they cannot make ends meet. An elderly woman spends her mornings teaching adults who never went to school to read, although on many days she comes home tired and disheartened. What would Camus say about each case? And Frankl? Which one meets Wolf's two conditions, and which one lacks one of them? Is any of them happy without their life having much meaning, or the other way round? Beware of hasty judgements: perhaps in video games there are friendships, effort and a team, and perhaps the boy finds meaning in helping his family even though the task bores him. Finally, think about yourself: if tomorrow you had to explain to someone what makes your life worth living, what would you say? Do you feel that you found it, chose it or were given it?</p>\n<p class=\"aporia\">To close the topic, choose one of three assignments. (1) <strong>Philosophical video</strong>: in a group, make a video of two or three minutes on a question from this topic; choose a genuine question, defend a clear thesis, use at least two concepts from the topic and take care over the sound. The best ones can be entered for the Philosophy Olympiad. (2) <strong>Film</strong>: in <em>La Chimera</em> (Alice Rohrwacher, 2023), a young man searches for treasure in Etruscan tombs, but what he is really looking for is a woman he has lost. What is his chimera, his impossible dream? If he were to achieve it, would he find meaning? Relate it to Frankl or Camus. (3) <strong>Opinion piece</strong>: write a page on a current conflict (a war, climate change, hatred on social media) analysed with two authors from the topic, and finish by saying which perspective you find more useful and why.</p>\n<p>Here the circle we opened at the beginning closes. We began by asking ourselves what the human being is and have seen that there is no single answer: we are a biological and cultural animal, body and mind, a being that asks about itself and that each tradition has understood in its own way. Answering “what am I?” and “what meaning do I want to give my life?” is not a task for an exam, but for a whole lifetime; and doing so with lucidity, with <strong>freedom</strong> and with a critical sense is, at bottom, to go on practising that <strong>philosophical attitude</strong> —the wonder that is not satisfied with easy answers— with which we began the course.</p>"}});
